1
00:00:42,139 --> 00:00:45,378
Please vacate
the building quickly.

2
00:00:48,619 --> 00:00:51,339
Do not use the lifts.

3
00:00:52,179 --> 00:00:56,729
Please leave at once,
this is a bomb alarm.

4
00:00:56,819 --> 00:01:01,258
- Is everyone out? Is everyone out?
- Officers are checking.

5
00:01:01,259 --> 00:01:04,339
Forgive me. No, David.

6
00:01:04,419 --> 00:01:06,753
Professor Blackthorn.

7
00:01:10,539 --> 00:01:12,979
- Clive!
- Go. Just go.

8
00:01:13,899 --> 00:01:15,019
Sir!

9
00:01:33,819 --> 00:01:36,069
Ah, John. You're just done?

10
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:38,778
Twenty thousand cases waiting
to be heard in the High Court,

11
00:01:38,779 --> 00:01:42,058
only 107 judges.
How that was allowed to run, I...

12
00:01:42,059 --> 00:01:45,938
Unfortunately, the come before judges
without your mettle.

13
00:01:45,939 --> 00:01:47,738
What's this? You sacked your clerk?

14
00:01:47,739 --> 00:01:50,258
I'm wondering if you'd take on
the animal rights bomber?

15
00:01:50,259 --> 00:01:52,898
Isn't the Attorney General
prosecuting that one?

16
00:01:52,899 --> 00:01:57,458
I'm growing a little tired of being
viewed as their safe pair of hands.

17
00:01:57,459 --> 00:02:00,178
He expects to win,
and that's why he's taken it on.

18
00:02:00,179 --> 00:02:04,098
I think they want to squash the Animal
Rights Movement once and for all.

19
00:02:04,099 --> 00:02:06,249
Well, no doubt it's time.

20
00:02:06,339 --> 00:02:08,258
They're still causing
an awful lot of mayhem.

21
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:09,299
Come in.

22
00:02:14,539 --> 00:02:15,609
Jo?

23
00:02:17,059 --> 00:02:20,098
I've just heard you're returning
the Henry Free brief, why?

24
00:02:20,099 --> 00:02:25,516
- He likes you, he trusts you.
- I know. Apologise to the client.

25
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:28,218
Is it because the Attorney General
is prosecuting?

26
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:33,553
Oh, not sure if it's winnable, Charlie,
but that's not my reason.

27
00:02:34,699 --> 00:02:36,858
Your dad is hearing the case.

28
00:02:36,859 --> 00:02:39,443
Things are changing in my life.

29
00:02:39,579 --> 00:02:43,698
I ought to speak to John,
I've been putting it off.

30
00:02:43,699 --> 00:02:46,618
Simon Norwalk is a good alternative QC.

31
00:02:46,619 --> 00:02:49,786
He's different from me, but he's good.

32
00:02:49,979 --> 00:02:51,178
Is anything wrong, Jo?

33
00:02:51,179 --> 00:02:53,178
Talk to the client
about the changes, Charlie.

34
00:02:53,179 --> 00:02:54,299
Reassure him.

35
00:02:59,579 --> 00:03:01,378
I prefer Mrs Mills.

36
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:03,378
Simon Norwalk is a good choice.

37
00:03:03,379 --> 00:03:06,178
- Does he even believe me?
- I believe you, Henry.

38
00:03:06,179 --> 00:03:08,738
- Then you defend me.
- You're on a murder charge.

39
00:03:08,739 --> 00:03:11,778
Someone set me up,
I didn't do that bombing.

40
00:03:11,779 --> 00:03:14,258
People that rescue animals
from vivisection

41
00:03:14,259 --> 00:03:15,738
don't blow up humans.

42
00:03:15,739 --> 00:03:17,058
Then let's use your alibi...

43
00:03:17,059 --> 00:03:18,978
Who'll give evidence to support it?

44
00:03:18,979 --> 00:03:21,778
Only people from the movement
can prove that I was there.

45
00:03:21,779 --> 00:03:23,778
If the come forward
they'll be arrested,

46
00:03:23,779 --> 00:03:27,858
all the animals we rescued
will be returned and killed.

47
00:03:27,859 --> 00:03:32,276
I'm not going to allow that to happen,
there's no way.

48
00:03:32,459 --> 00:03:33,793
Go with Charlie.

49
00:03:35,099 --> 00:03:37,349
I'm not experienced enough.

50
00:03:41,019 --> 00:03:43,103
Sir Ian Rochester, Judge.

51
00:03:43,139 --> 00:03:44,259
Thanks, Coop.

52
00:03:45,099 --> 00:03:48,699
Ian, let me guess why you're here.

53
00:03:48,779 --> 00:03:53,029
- Would you like some tea?
- That would be rev nice.

54
00:03:54,979 --> 00:03:58,018
Would be of a mind
to help the government out

55
00:03:58,019 --> 00:04:00,603
with the science block bombing?

56
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:02,858
That would depend entirely
on what the government wants.

57
00:04:02,859 --> 00:04:04,618
Help yourself to milk and sugar.

58
00:04:04,619 --> 00:04:08,098
The identity of the main witness
must not be revealed,

59
00:04:08,099 --> 00:04:10,058
having penetrated
the Animal Rights Movement.

60
00:04:10,059 --> 00:04:13,658
Yes, I'd thought I'd agreed that
under Public Interest Immunity.

61
00:04:13,659 --> 00:04:17,076
Your views on PII are well known,
my lord.

62
00:04:25,659 --> 00:04:30,218
You know, I'm not really happy about you
going to see the Judge on your own.

63
00:04:30,219 --> 00:04:31,259
Oh!

64
00:04:31,739 --> 00:04:33,573
He might win you back.

65
00:04:34,059 --> 00:04:38,309
Would you, um...
Would you like me to come with you?

66
00:04:38,659 --> 00:04:39,779
What?

67
00:04:40,499 --> 00:04:42,418
I don't think John would like that.

68
00:04:42,419 --> 00:04:43,618
I could put off my meeting.

69
00:04:43,619 --> 00:04:47,369
You need that extra money
for your department.

70
00:04:48,619 --> 00:04:53,786
- Um, it's marked the table.
- Oh! I'm sorry, I'll get a cloth.

71
00:04:56,139 --> 00:04:59,018
I wondered if I might see you again.

72
00:04:59,019 --> 00:05:02,178
You've returned the brief
in the bombing case.

73
00:05:02,179 --> 00:05:04,263
I thought it best, John.

74
00:05:04,739 --> 00:05:08,258
You've never let personal relations
get in the way of law.

75
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:10,338
There have been some sharp remarks
from the bench.

76
00:05:10,339 --> 00:05:12,673
Riposte from the floor, too.

77
00:05:13,699 --> 00:05:17,866
I couldn't rely on
our even-handedness continuing.

78
00:05:18,179 --> 00:05:20,096
You overstate the case.

79
00:05:23,699 --> 00:05:25,138
Do you want some tea?

80
00:05:25,139 --> 00:05:28,418
- I've been drinking it all morning.
- Yeah, me, too.

81
00:05:28,419 --> 00:05:30,619
- Alcohol?
- No, bit earl.

82
00:05:32,259 --> 00:05:35,509
All right, uh... small brand.

83
00:05:51,539 --> 00:05:55,956
This is beginning to feel like
something it shouldn't.

84
00:05:59,219 --> 00:06:00,886
Do you want another?

85
00:06:12,739 --> 00:06:14,156
Jo, what's wrong?

86
00:06:14,219 --> 00:06:16,698
Nothing. I just wanted
to explain the returned brief.

87
00:06:16,699 --> 00:06:19,698
Oh. Does this mean
I can see you socially?

88
00:06:19,699 --> 00:06:21,699
You have been down here.

89
00:06:22,219 --> 00:06:25,386
I have been in town quite a lot, too.

90
00:06:26,819 --> 00:06:30,569
I told you, John,
I'm not sure what I feel.

91
00:06:32,779 --> 00:06:34,196
It's hard for me.

92
00:06:38,059 --> 00:06:42,498
You struggle to get to
A certain position in life and then

93
00:06:42,499 --> 00:06:48,179
it all seems so unimportant
without a meaningful relationship.

94
00:06:50,179 --> 00:06:52,898
Suddenly, I feel you're like sand
running through my fingers

95
00:06:52,899 --> 00:06:55,899
and I can't do anything to stop it.

96
00:06:58,179 --> 00:07:00,929
- What's happening, Jo?
- Nothing.

97
00:07:01,499 --> 00:07:03,416
Nothing I can control.

98
00:07:04,779 --> 00:07:07,419
Where are we as of now?

99
00:07:14,539 --> 00:07:19,373
I need more time to sort things out.
Can you give me time?

100
00:07:19,779 --> 00:07:21,139
Yes, of course.

101
00:07:23,659 --> 00:07:25,578
It was jolly a good skeleton.

102
00:07:25,579 --> 00:07:28,058
Jonathan Primatt,
the instructing solicitor, helped.

103
00:07:28,059 --> 00:07:30,778
What are you telling me,
you're not as good as I thought?

104
00:07:30,779 --> 00:07:34,018
The prosecution will find
an chink in your defence.

105
00:07:34,019 --> 00:07:35,778
I thought it worth a submission
for dismissal

106
00:07:35,779 --> 00:07:37,863
before the jury is sworn.

107
00:07:39,459 --> 00:07:41,658
The Attorney General
didn't take this case on

108
00:07:41,659 --> 00:07:44,539
to fall at the first hurdle. Hmm?

109
00:07:55,939 --> 00:07:57,538
Alan, what a nice surprise.

110
00:07:57,539 --> 00:07:58,618
Simon, my dear fellow.

111
00:07:58,619 --> 00:08:00,338
I'm sure now it will be
far more agreeable

112
00:08:00,339 --> 00:08:02,098
than it would have been with Mrs Mills.

113
00:08:02,099 --> 00:08:03,618
Peter, how are you?

114
00:08:03,619 --> 00:08:07,119
There's ghastly toenail pie
in the canteen.

115
00:08:07,739 --> 00:08:09,073
All rise.

116
00:08:17,539 --> 00:08:21,978
I noticed a number of animal rights
protestors outside when I arrived,

117
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:24,738
I dare say
there are some here this morning.

118
00:08:24,739 --> 00:08:26,258
This is a public court,

119
00:08:26,259 --> 00:08:29,593
but I will not tolerate
an disturbances.

120
00:08:30,299 --> 00:08:32,178
Other than from your barristers.

121
00:08:32,179 --> 00:08:33,858
Mr Attorney, it's good to see you again.

122
00:08:33,859 --> 00:08:35,338
Ah, likewise, my lord.

123
00:08:35,339 --> 00:08:37,258
Mr Norwalk, am I going to hear
from you at this stage?

124
00:08:37,259 --> 00:08:39,093
I think not, my lord.

125
00:08:40,139 --> 00:08:42,338
- Yes, Sir Alan?
- There are matters arising

126
00:08:42,339 --> 00:08:44,298
in the House of Commons, my lord,

127
00:08:44,299 --> 00:08:46,458
which I shall have to deal with
from time to time.

128
00:08:46,459 --> 00:08:48,018
Sir Peter is with me,

129
00:08:48,019 --> 00:08:51,218
and will take over on such occasions
with your leave, my lord?

130
00:08:51,219 --> 00:08:54,178
- I'm sure you'll tip-toe out.
- Yes.

131
00:08:54,179 --> 00:08:57,578
There's also a question of immunity
in this case,

132
00:08:57,579 --> 00:09:00,578
in respect to
the main prosecution witness, my lord.

133
00:09:00,579 --> 00:09:03,738
The prosecution would like this
to remain in place for the entirety

134
00:09:03,739 --> 00:09:05,018
and after the trial.

135
00:09:05,019 --> 00:09:06,978
Yes, I did agree at the ex parte hearing

136
00:09:06,979 --> 00:09:08,978
to this witness's identity
being withheld,

137
00:09:08,979 --> 00:09:11,618
but it will depend
upon the circumstances.

138
00:09:11,619 --> 00:09:13,418
Would you alert me when you intend
to call the witness?

139
00:09:13,419 --> 00:09:14,538
- Yes.
- Thank you.

140
00:09:14,539 --> 00:09:16,658
Are we read to swear a jury?

141
00:09:16,659 --> 00:09:18,658
Yes? Thank you. Mr Harrison.

142
00:09:18,659 --> 00:09:22,858
Members of the jury, the facts
in a murder trial are never simple

143
00:09:22,859 --> 00:09:27,058
but I will endeavour to
la them out clearly for you.

144
00:09:27,059 --> 00:09:29,939
At 2:45pm,

145
00:09:30,019 --> 00:09:32,379
on February the 11 Th last,

146
00:09:32,459 --> 00:09:35,058
Sussex police received
A telephone warning

147
00:09:35,059 --> 00:09:38,978
that a bomb had been planted
in the brand new animal hotel

148
00:09:38,979 --> 00:09:42,259
at Sussex University science faculty.

149
00:09:43,299 --> 00:09:46,979
The caller said the bomb
would go off at 3pm.

150
00:09:47,939 --> 00:09:53,538
The police alerted the university,
who began to evacuate the building.

151
00:09:53,539 --> 00:09:56,458
Now, one of the occupants
of the building,

152
00:09:56,459 --> 00:09:59,259
Professor Clive Blackthorn,

153
00:09:59,339 --> 00:10:05,779
A professor of neuropathy, ad
left the building by 2:47pm.

154
00:10:06,979 --> 00:10:10,978
Then, having forgotten some
irreplaceable papers in his research

155
00:10:10,979 --> 00:10:14,618
into Parkinson's disease, he
rushed back into the building.

156
00:10:14,619 --> 00:10:17,458
He caught the full blast of the bomb,

157
00:10:17,459 --> 00:10:21,018
so callously timed
to go off 10 minutes earlier

158
00:10:21,019 --> 00:10:24,178
than the time given
in the original warning.

159
00:10:24,179 --> 00:10:27,898
Now, Professor Blackthorn
ad, for a long time,

160
00:10:27,899 --> 00:10:31,233
been a persistent object
of vilification,

161
00:10:31,339 --> 00:10:36,498
but he refused to be intimidated
and carried on with his vital work,

162
00:10:36,499 --> 00:10:41,249
suffering as he did
from various stress-related illnesses.

163
00:10:41,819 --> 00:10:45,419
It was this life, dedicated to science,

164
00:10:46,139 --> 00:10:51,639
that was stolen by the man who stands
before you in the dock today.

165
00:10:51,979 --> 00:10:53,809
Mr Henry Free,

166
00:10:54,659 --> 00:10:59,939
the self-appointed leader of ARM,
the Animal Rights Movement.

167
00:11:01,099 --> 00:11:04,938
This explosion so damaged
this multi-million pound building

168
00:11:04,939 --> 00:11:06,658
that it had to be pulled down.

169
00:11:06,659 --> 00:11:10,099
Saving lots of animals' lives.

170
00:11:10,179 --> 00:11:11,579
Who called out?

171
00:11:15,019 --> 00:11:18,418
You presumably have a great deal
of empathy with the animals?

172
00:11:18,419 --> 00:11:19,939
Yes, I do.

173
00:11:21,299 --> 00:11:24,259
- What's your name?
- Joan Crick.

174
00:11:24,499 --> 00:11:25,499
Thank you.

175
00:11:26,579 --> 00:11:29,339
Well, what's going to happen now

176
00:11:29,419 --> 00:11:32,338
is that the usher
will escort you downstairs

177
00:11:32,339 --> 00:11:35,778
and you will remain there
until the court is adjourned.

178
00:11:35,779 --> 00:11:41,099
The animals are sentenced to life,
and then a rev painful death.

179
00:11:41,179 --> 00:11:42,179
Usher.

180
00:11:46,419 --> 00:11:49,619
Now, then, if anyone else interrupts,

181
00:11:49,699 --> 00:11:53,258
the will go to the same place,
and I will clear the court.

182
00:11:53,259 --> 00:11:55,509
Make no mistake about that.

183
00:11:56,019 --> 00:11:57,019
Mr Attorney.

184
00:11:59,379 --> 00:12:02,658
One life was callously
and calculatingly taken,

185
00:12:02,659 --> 00:12:07,258
and now man people may never find
relief from their crippling illnesses.

186
00:12:07,259 --> 00:12:11,499
Animal testing is a fraud!
It's a bad science!

187
00:12:12,139 --> 00:12:17,258
Look, protest is a legitimate activity
in a free and democratic society,

188
00:12:17,259 --> 00:12:19,738
but I can't allow it in court.

189
00:12:19,739 --> 00:12:21,218
Now, you will remain seated,

190
00:12:21,219 --> 00:12:24,058
everybody else who has no function here
please leave immediately.

191
00:12:24,059 --> 00:12:26,476
Right now, go on, all of you.

192
00:12:38,339 --> 00:12:41,018
- What's your name?
- Martin House.

193
00:12:41,019 --> 00:12:42,853
I'm a nursing officer.

194
00:12:43,219 --> 00:12:46,138
And it's shameful that this government

195
00:12:46,139 --> 00:12:49,098
gives those cowards money
to torture animals.

196
00:12:49,099 --> 00:12:52,849
Yes, thank you.
Dock officer, remove Mr House.

197
00:12:57,219 --> 00:13:01,849
You see, these issues
arouse great passions,

198
00:13:01,939 --> 00:13:05,018
but what you must keep in mind
is that you are not here

199
00:13:05,019 --> 00:13:08,178
to consider whether it is right or wrong
to experiment on animals.

200
00:13:08,179 --> 00:13:11,378
You are here to decide whether
the defendant is guilt

201
00:13:11,379 --> 00:13:13,339
or not guilt of murder.

202
00:13:14,339 --> 00:13:19,506
And this you must decide solely
on the evidence, is that clear?

203
00:13:20,819 --> 00:13:23,499
Yes? Sure? Okay.

204
00:13:23,579 --> 00:13:24,859
Mr Attorney.

205
00:13:26,659 --> 00:13:30,939
The prosecution will show
that the man in the dock,

206
00:13:31,019 --> 00:13:33,138
acting in concert with others,

207
00:13:33,139 --> 00:13:38,779
made and planted this devastating bomb
in the name of animal rights,

208
00:13:39,939 --> 00:13:44,019
a cause as bizarre
as is it is misnamed.

209
00:13:45,419 --> 00:13:51,259
In our country, under our laws,
animals have no rights.

210
00:13:52,179 --> 00:13:57,579
And if the did, would the want murder
done in their name?

211
00:13:57,659 --> 00:14:02,049
Well, the murder millions of animals
in whose name?

212
00:14:02,139 --> 00:14:06,658
Mr Free, interruptions of this kind
will see you removed as well.

213
00:14:06,659 --> 00:14:11,243
Now, please make an communications
through your counsel.

214
00:14:11,939 --> 00:14:13,023
Please.

215
00:14:15,659 --> 00:14:16,778
Chief Inspector,

216
00:14:16,779 --> 00:14:21,058
I believe you conducted the hunt
for the animal hotel bombers?

217
00:14:21,059 --> 00:14:24,099
Yes, with the anti-terrorist unit.

218
00:14:24,179 --> 00:14:27,458
Oh. Was this thought to be the work
of Al-Qaida?

219
00:14:27,459 --> 00:14:31,218
Animal rights activists
are considered terrorists.

220
00:14:31,219 --> 00:14:32,658
Can the be so dangerous?

221
00:14:32,659 --> 00:14:37,458
In terms of terrorism, the are
considered second only to Al-Qaida.

222
00:14:37,459 --> 00:14:41,898
They've done millions of pounds'
worth of damage to laboratories,

223
00:14:41,899 --> 00:14:45,898
run all-out campaigns of terror
against the people who work there.

224
00:14:45,899 --> 00:14:50,458
And are animal rights activists
categorised as fanatical?

225
00:14:50,459 --> 00:14:51,819
Some are.

226
00:14:51,899 --> 00:14:55,298
Presumably a large number
are law-abiding?

227
00:14:55,299 --> 00:14:57,498
Yes, sir. The vast majority are.

228
00:14:57,499 --> 00:15:00,778
But the ones who blew up
the university building are not.

229
00:15:00,779 --> 00:15:05,338
No, sir. We pursued the line
that the were professional.

230
00:15:05,339 --> 00:15:06,459
Ruthless.

231
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:08,058
Did it produce results?

232
00:15:08,059 --> 00:15:11,819
Not quickly, it's a tight-knit cell.

233
00:15:11,899 --> 00:15:14,499
Ordinary, talk is loose.

234
00:15:14,579 --> 00:15:17,418
Activists bragging about
what they've done.

235
00:15:17,419 --> 00:15:18,698
These were fanatics?

236
00:15:18,699 --> 00:15:21,779
- Oh, really, my lord.
- Mr Attorney,

237
00:15:21,859 --> 00:15:23,738
the Chief Inspector
doesn't need leading.

238
00:15:23,739 --> 00:15:25,138
I apologise, my lord.

239
00:15:25,139 --> 00:15:29,498
Chief Inspector, well, how would you,
then, categorise the persons

240
00:15:29,499 --> 00:15:32,978
- who carried out this bombing?
- Fanatical.

241
00:15:32,979 --> 00:15:38,479
That was the advice received
from an expert psychological profiler.

242
00:15:38,779 --> 00:15:43,818
Was that profile drawn after Mr Free
was identified as a suspect

243
00:15:43,819 --> 00:15:46,099
or did it lead you to him?

244
00:15:46,179 --> 00:15:49,618
We drew up a list of people
who fitted the profile.

245
00:15:49,619 --> 00:15:51,769
The defendant was on it.

246
00:15:51,859 --> 00:15:55,738
But was he sufficiently well-matched
in profile pegs

247
00:15:55,739 --> 00:15:58,699
for you to say "He's your man"?

248
00:15:59,619 --> 00:16:03,418
He had as man pegs
as other potential suspects.

249
00:16:03,419 --> 00:16:06,138
- Were the charged?
- Only the defendant.

250
00:16:06,139 --> 00:16:09,218
Well, how did that come about,
if he was no higher in profile pegs

251
00:16:09,219 --> 00:16:10,538
than anyone else?

252
00:16:10,539 --> 00:16:13,458
- We interviewed him, sir.
- Did he admit he was involved?

253
00:16:13,459 --> 00:16:14,859
He did not.

254
00:16:14,939 --> 00:16:19,139
Then what made him the prime suspect?

255
00:16:19,219 --> 00:16:23,339
He had no alibi for
when the bomb was planted.

256
00:16:23,419 --> 00:16:25,489
We searched his home,

257
00:16:25,579 --> 00:16:29,258
we found items
that could be included in a bomb.

258
00:16:29,259 --> 00:16:32,658
Were those items found in the bomb
at the university?

259
00:16:32,659 --> 00:16:34,978
- Some were.
- Forensically matched

260
00:16:34,979 --> 00:16:37,498
to those items taken from his flat?

261
00:16:37,499 --> 00:16:39,939
No. The were similar.

262
00:16:41,419 --> 00:16:43,658
Let's look at some these items,
shall we?

263
00:16:43,659 --> 00:16:45,493
Usher, would you mind?

264
00:16:47,379 --> 00:16:48,609
Matches.

265
00:16:51,179 --> 00:16:53,418
Would you show them to the jury?

266
00:16:53,419 --> 00:16:57,098
It's even possible the jury
as similar matches at home.

267
00:16:57,099 --> 00:16:58,778
Would you care to test that, Mr Norwalk?

268
00:16:58,779 --> 00:17:01,898
I think the jury gets the picture,
my lord.

269
00:17:01,899 --> 00:17:03,379
Batteries.

270
00:17:03,459 --> 00:17:06,418
The sort we all have in your TV remotes.

271
00:17:06,419 --> 00:17:07,649
Bell wire.

272
00:17:08,459 --> 00:17:11,139
A book on electrical circuits,

273
00:17:12,099 --> 00:17:15,099
another one on bombs.

274
00:17:15,179 --> 00:17:18,658
Now, if this were a trial
for conspiracy to make explosives,

275
00:17:18,659 --> 00:17:21,379
that might be damning evidence.

276
00:17:21,459 --> 00:17:23,178
What sort of a bomb was it?

277
00:17:23,179 --> 00:17:25,258
I'm not a bomb expert, sir.

278
00:17:25,259 --> 00:17:28,258
Major Klanner of
the Royal Ordnance Corps

279
00:17:28,259 --> 00:17:31,458
stated in his report
it was highly sophisticated.

280
00:17:31,459 --> 00:17:34,499
In fact, it was so sophisticated

281
00:17:34,579 --> 00:17:39,739
it had a timing device accurate
to one thousandth of a second.

282
00:17:41,339 --> 00:17:44,809
Detonated by nitroglycerine.

283
00:17:47,339 --> 00:17:50,458
Not really the sort of contraption
cooked up on the kitchen table

284
00:17:50,459 --> 00:17:52,618
- by an amateur.
- The defendant

285
00:17:52,619 --> 00:17:55,018
has a degree in physics, sir.

286
00:17:55,019 --> 00:17:56,899
Well, so did Einstein.

287
00:17:58,339 --> 00:18:00,338
I bet he couldn't have put
one of these bombs together

288
00:18:00,339 --> 00:18:02,178
on the kitchen table, either.

289
00:18:02,179 --> 00:18:06,649
Mr Free also had nitroglycerine
on his jumper, sir.

290
00:18:06,739 --> 00:18:08,298
From my internet search,

291
00:18:08,299 --> 00:18:13,498
there are almost 600 different products
with nitroglycerine traces in them.

292
00:18:13,499 --> 00:18:16,098
It was only nitroglycerine traces found?

293
00:18:16,099 --> 00:18:21,618
He couldn't give an account of where
he might have picked up such traces.

294
00:18:21,619 --> 00:18:25,379
An more than an one of us could.

295
00:18:26,659 --> 00:18:30,129
So we come again
to how Mr Free

296
00:18:30,219 --> 00:18:34,053
figured so prominently
in the list of suspects?

297
00:18:38,819 --> 00:18:42,403
You know my earlier ruling,
Chief Inspector.

298
00:18:47,899 --> 00:18:50,983
I can't take this matter an further.

299
00:18:51,979 --> 00:18:55,369
Well, in that case, neither can I.

300
00:18:59,019 --> 00:19:02,138
Then we'd better take this in chambers.

301
00:19:02,139 --> 00:19:06,723
You might like to stretch your legs
and get some coffee.

302
00:19:08,659 --> 00:19:12,618
Thank you, Chief Inspector,
you can step down for now.

303
00:19:12,619 --> 00:19:13,619
Charlie.

304
00:19:17,339 --> 00:19:18,839
What's this about?

305
00:19:18,859 --> 00:19:21,498
He's to protect the identity
of the informant.

306
00:19:21,499 --> 00:19:22,738
Do we get the identity?

307
00:19:22,739 --> 00:19:25,618
No, he'll be behind a screen
and the voice disguised.

308
00:19:25,619 --> 00:19:27,219
That's outrageous.

309
00:19:27,619 --> 00:19:29,738
Look, it can't be anyone
from the movement.

310
00:19:29,739 --> 00:19:33,073
I'll explain as and when I learn more.

311
00:19:40,339 --> 00:19:43,649
I'm never happy about PII, Sir Peter.

312
00:19:44,419 --> 00:19:46,618
It smacks of state advantage.

313
00:19:46,619 --> 00:19:50,498
Hardly. It is necessary,
especially as terrorism is involved.

314
00:19:50,499 --> 00:19:54,298
The first issue is whether
an informant's identity needs protecting

315
00:19:54,299 --> 00:19:56,018
in these circumstances, my lord.

316
00:19:56,019 --> 00:19:58,818
Protestors have terrorised staff
at the science faculty

317
00:19:58,819 --> 00:20:00,618
and the builders,
despite the change in the law.

318
00:20:00,619 --> 00:20:04,409
Yes, but PII
does disadvantage the defence.

319
00:20:05,899 --> 00:20:09,018
Animal rights cells are becoming harder
and harder to infiltrate.

320
00:20:09,019 --> 00:20:10,298
This person having gained entry,

321
00:20:10,299 --> 00:20:12,458
the police are keen
to preserve his identity.

322
00:20:12,459 --> 00:20:14,898
At the risk of a miscarriage of justice.

323
00:20:14,899 --> 00:20:16,618
Well, that defeats the object entirely.

324
00:20:16,619 --> 00:20:19,978
The credentials of the source protected
by the PII are impeccable.

325
00:20:19,979 --> 00:20:25,229
That's difficult to assess
when I've no idea who it is.

326
00:20:30,179 --> 00:20:35,938
That's something I'm going to have to
investigate in your absence, Mr Norwalk.

327
00:20:35,939 --> 00:20:40,179
My real concern is
why only Mr Free is in the dock.

328
00:20:40,259 --> 00:20:42,593
Yes, again, I will enquire.

329
00:20:45,339 --> 00:20:46,619
Charlie.

330
00:20:50,419 --> 00:20:53,669
How reliable is your witness, Sir Alan?

331
00:20:53,819 --> 00:20:55,258
Well, he is copper-bottom, my lord.

332
00:20:55,259 --> 00:20:58,978
As you know, he works for MI5,
you can't get more reliable than that.

333
00:20:58,979 --> 00:21:03,938
I know the sometimes get frustrated
and find a means to the end.

334
00:21:03,939 --> 00:21:05,769
I hardly think so.

335
00:21:05,859 --> 00:21:08,018
The reason this informant
is thought so highly of

336
00:21:08,019 --> 00:21:10,898
is that he won't round up
vast swathes of suspects

337
00:21:10,899 --> 00:21:13,658
only to have them walk away from court
through lack of evidence.

338
00:21:13,659 --> 00:21:15,418
He's still going to have to give
live evidence.

339
00:21:15,419 --> 00:21:16,489
Well, fine.

340
00:21:16,579 --> 00:21:19,658
As long as he is protected,
we'd sooner offer the defendant

341
00:21:19,659 --> 00:21:22,218
the opportunity to plead guilt
to manslaughter

342
00:21:22,219 --> 00:21:26,053
than risk the informant's identity
being known.

343
00:21:26,779 --> 00:21:31,649
Was this MI5 person present
during illegal activity?

344
00:21:31,739 --> 00:21:35,489
He was when the defendant
planned the bombing.

345
00:21:37,139 --> 00:21:41,973
Well, I still dislike
evidence being given behind screens.

346
00:21:42,339 --> 00:21:44,778
But there is a balance to be struck

347
00:21:44,779 --> 00:21:50,529
between the defendant's right
to a fair trial and the public interest.

348
00:21:50,979 --> 00:21:53,258
And in this case I accept
the public interest lies

349
00:21:53,259 --> 00:21:57,578
in protecting a valuable source
of police intelligence.

350
00:21:57,579 --> 00:22:00,818
All right, I shall get
the defence team back.

351
00:22:00,819 --> 00:22:02,153
All rise.

352
00:22:10,259 --> 00:22:13,818
He's not changing his original ruling,
Simon, no further details.

353
00:22:13,819 --> 00:22:16,658
The witness will be shielded
from your client by screens.

354
00:22:16,659 --> 00:22:18,738
An objections to the precise procedure?

355
00:22:18,739 --> 00:22:20,498
Can we have the jury back?

356
00:22:20,499 --> 00:22:22,999
I will try to out him, Peter.

357
00:22:23,299 --> 00:22:26,258
Alan would sooner let him plead guilt
to manslaughter

358
00:22:26,259 --> 00:22:27,978
than have that happen.

359
00:22:27,979 --> 00:22:30,646
Is manslaughter an option, Alan?

360
00:22:36,459 --> 00:22:38,698
Plead guilt? I didn't do it.

361
00:22:38,699 --> 00:22:41,418
- The have an impeccable witness.
- Who is it?

362
00:22:41,419 --> 00:22:43,738
You know the rules of PII,
we can't get the name.

363
00:22:43,739 --> 00:22:45,378
Whoever it is is ling.

364
00:22:45,379 --> 00:22:47,629
This source is well-placed.

365
00:22:48,619 --> 00:22:50,138
Henry didn't do that bombing.

366
00:22:50,139 --> 00:22:52,298
Well, then, he has to provide his alibi.

367
00:22:52,299 --> 00:22:55,133
I was rescuing animals elsewhere.

368
00:22:56,819 --> 00:22:59,538
I'd sooner go to prison
than betray anyone in the movement.

369
00:22:59,539 --> 00:23:02,138
Well, someone in the movement
is clearly betraying you, Mr Free.

370
00:23:02,139 --> 00:23:03,899
There's no way.

371
00:23:03,979 --> 00:23:07,418
Just you show the jury what happens
to animals in those laboratories.

372
00:23:07,419 --> 00:23:08,618
See who the believe.

373
00:23:08,619 --> 00:23:11,618
That would suggest we're running
A defence justifying your actions.

374
00:23:11,619 --> 00:23:13,036
I can't do that.

375
00:23:13,059 --> 00:23:14,858
Well, I want you to show them.

376
00:23:14,859 --> 00:23:17,819
You will be the loser, Mr Free.

377
00:23:17,899 --> 00:23:19,858
Are we supposed to accept
this person's lies

378
00:23:19,859 --> 00:23:21,858
just because the prosecution says so?

379
00:23:21,859 --> 00:23:26,099
No. We refute them, we counter them,

380
00:23:26,179 --> 00:23:28,763
then see who the jury believes.

381
00:23:29,779 --> 00:23:32,538
I've known Sir Alan Peasmarsh
A long while.

382
00:23:32,539 --> 00:23:35,379
He doesn't knowingly tell lies.

383
00:23:35,459 --> 00:23:37,179
Well, someone is.

384
00:23:37,939 --> 00:23:42,778
I'm sure Sir Alan didn't take this on
believing you had a knockout alibi.

385
00:23:42,779 --> 00:23:44,978
Have a talk with your solicitor.

386
00:23:44,979 --> 00:23:46,979
Listen carefully to him.

387
00:23:47,859 --> 00:23:48,859
Thank you.

388
00:23:52,819 --> 00:23:56,258
- Is he working for the prosecution?
- He's right, Henry.

389
00:23:56,259 --> 00:23:58,658
Look, I don't want him
to represent me an more.

390
00:23:58,659 --> 00:24:00,379
I don't trust him.

391
00:24:00,459 --> 00:24:03,618
Look how friendly he is
with that government creep.

392
00:24:03,619 --> 00:24:08,058
I want complete confidence in
the person speaking for me in court.

393
00:24:08,059 --> 00:24:09,618
I have confidence in Charlie.

394
00:24:09,619 --> 00:24:12,058
- Charlie is not a leader.
- But she's done all the work.

395
00:24:12,059 --> 00:24:13,778
This is a murder case.

396
00:24:13,779 --> 00:24:16,258
He's right, Henry, you need a proper QC.

397
00:24:16,259 --> 00:24:21,009
Look, either you do it, Charlie,
or I'll represent myself.

398
00:24:23,779 --> 00:24:24,929
Come in.

399
00:24:25,219 --> 00:24:28,469
- Mr Thompson, miss.
- Thank you, Terry.

400
00:24:29,699 --> 00:24:31,498
Did you get good news?

401
00:24:31,499 --> 00:24:33,178
Department of Health liked my proposals,

402
00:24:33,179 --> 00:24:36,138
only come up
with four million extra funding.

403
00:24:36,139 --> 00:24:38,209
- Ah!
- Hang on.

404
00:24:38,299 --> 00:24:40,218
- Hmm?
- Where's the cheque?

405
00:24:40,219 --> 00:24:42,018
- Oh, you have to read the small print.
- Oh.

406
00:24:42,019 --> 00:24:43,258
I never read the small print.

407
00:24:43,259 --> 00:24:45,258
There's another committee.
To get the money,

408
00:24:45,259 --> 00:24:49,298
I have to present
my estimated patient throughputs

409
00:24:49,299 --> 00:24:52,338
and get out of bed the right side
three consecutive mornings.

410
00:24:52,339 --> 00:24:55,498
- What's the right side?
- The opposite of the wrong side.

411
00:24:55,499 --> 00:24:57,858
I thought you left that sense of humour
in South Africa?

412
00:24:57,859 --> 00:25:00,498
Can I have an affidavit saying
I got out of bed the right side?

413
00:25:00,499 --> 00:25:02,658
I haven't slept with you
for three consecutive nights.

414
00:25:02,659 --> 00:25:05,499
No. You should remedy that.

415
00:25:07,859 --> 00:25:09,899
Jo, I... Oh!

416
00:25:09,979 --> 00:25:11,898
Oh, I'm sorry. I thought you were alone.

417
00:25:11,899 --> 00:25:16,578
That's all right, Marc's just going.
Marc Thompson, Charlie Deed.

418
00:25:16,579 --> 00:25:17,818
I can come back later.

419
00:25:17,819 --> 00:25:21,978
No, that's all right, I was just...
I was just on my way.

420
00:25:21,979 --> 00:25:23,378
- Tonight?
- I'll call you later.

421
00:25:23,379 --> 00:25:24,449
Okay.

422
00:25:26,099 --> 00:25:27,219
Excuse me.

423
00:25:30,499 --> 00:25:32,018
Didn't he go to South Africa?

424
00:25:32,019 --> 00:25:34,498
He's back. He got a job
at St James's, Brighton.

425
00:25:34,499 --> 00:25:35,499
Oh.

426
00:25:37,539 --> 00:25:39,898
- I haven't told your dad yet.
- Nothing to do with me.

427
00:25:39,899 --> 00:25:42,066
I hadn't got round to it.

428
00:25:43,099 --> 00:25:45,458
Look, I wanted to talk
to you about Henry Free.

429
00:25:45,459 --> 00:25:47,018
He wants to sack Simon Norwalk.

430
00:25:47,019 --> 00:25:49,058
- But Simon's very good.
- Hmm, doesn't believe he's innocent.

431
00:25:49,059 --> 00:25:50,458
He doesn't have to, Charlie.

432
00:25:50,459 --> 00:25:52,618
He suggested Henry
plead to manslaughter,

433
00:25:52,619 --> 00:25:53,698
that's what really did it.

434
00:25:53,699 --> 00:25:55,698
Was the Attorney offering that?

435
00:25:55,699 --> 00:25:57,769
The thing is, Jo, um,

436
00:25:57,859 --> 00:26:01,218
would you take it on?
I mean, you're across it all.

437
00:26:01,219 --> 00:26:03,378
I don't think I can, Charlie,
for all the same reasons.

438
00:26:03,379 --> 00:26:06,963
- Why, because Marc's back?
- Of course not.

439
00:26:08,979 --> 00:26:12,063
Persuade Mr Free to stick with Simon.

440
00:26:24,539 --> 00:26:27,178
- What a pleasant surprise.
- Did Charlie call you?

441
00:26:27,179 --> 00:26:28,859
Are you coming in?

442
00:26:28,939 --> 00:26:31,658
I thought she might have said something.

443
00:26:31,659 --> 00:26:33,243
Should I call her?

444
00:26:34,939 --> 00:26:37,658
Could you try anything as socially risk
as a cup of coffee?

445
00:26:37,659 --> 00:26:40,498
Her defendant wants
to sack Simon Norwalk.

446
00:26:40,499 --> 00:26:43,999
Oh! Thought I detected
A chill in the air.

447
00:26:44,619 --> 00:26:47,203
- You taking over?
- Not likely.

448
00:26:50,579 --> 00:26:52,858
- Simon Norwalk's very solid.
- Marc's back.

449
00:26:52,859 --> 00:26:54,298
What? He quit university?

450
00:26:54,299 --> 00:26:56,549
Not my Marc, Marc Thompson.

451
00:26:59,219 --> 00:27:00,219
Ah.

452
00:27:03,539 --> 00:27:08,373
Suddenly everything becomes rev clear,
why didn't I guess?

453
00:27:09,619 --> 00:27:14,369
Is he here on holiday with the children?
Michael with him?

454
00:27:14,579 --> 00:27:17,663
- Have you seen him?
- Michael's fine.

455
00:27:24,179 --> 00:27:27,578
And you didn't tell me,
just let me make a fool of myself.

456
00:27:27,579 --> 00:27:28,978
What, saying how much you love me?

457
00:27:28,979 --> 00:27:31,938
While sneak off to see somebody else.

458
00:27:31,939 --> 00:27:34,523
It is what you do all the time.

459
00:27:37,779 --> 00:27:39,539
It's so unlike you.

460
00:27:42,459 --> 00:27:43,959
Hurts, doesn't it?

461
00:27:48,459 --> 00:27:53,418
- How long is he here for?
- He's moved back here permanently.

462
00:27:53,419 --> 00:27:54,489
Oh!

463
00:27:58,699 --> 00:28:00,298
And now you want to throw it all away?

464
00:28:00,299 --> 00:28:02,419
No, of course not, John.

465
00:28:03,499 --> 00:28:04,938
That's what it sounds like.

466
00:28:04,939 --> 00:28:06,458
I don't want to lose you, John,
you're my best friend,

467
00:28:06,459 --> 00:28:09,709
I don't know what I'd do without you.

468
00:28:15,219 --> 00:28:19,053
Can't you give me more time
to sort things out?

469
00:28:20,019 --> 00:28:23,353
It seems to me that it's already sorted.

470
00:28:38,019 --> 00:28:41,218
The client has a right
to sack his barrister, Charlie,

471
00:28:41,219 --> 00:28:43,058
but he's a fool
if he instructs you to lead.

472
00:28:43,059 --> 00:28:45,458
- As you would be to accept.
- Well, I do know the case.

473
00:28:45,459 --> 00:28:47,698
Well, then, get your leader
to be more effective.

474
00:28:47,699 --> 00:28:50,498
- Henry doesn't want that.
- Who's Henry?

475
00:28:50,499 --> 00:28:52,099
The defendant.

476
00:28:52,179 --> 00:28:55,258
You are managing to maintain
some professional detachment, I hope?

477
00:28:55,259 --> 00:28:56,779
It was a slip.

478
00:28:56,859 --> 00:28:59,378
Anyway, you said that a committed
barrister was more effective.

479
00:28:59,379 --> 00:29:01,938
- Not if she's blinded by emotion.
- It doesn't happen with Jo.

480
00:29:01,939 --> 00:29:04,818
Well, then ask your
instructing solicitor to talk to her.

481
00:29:04,819 --> 00:29:07,018
She won't take the brief
because she'd be before you.

482
00:29:07,019 --> 00:29:08,936
She's so narrow-minded.

483
00:29:08,979 --> 00:29:11,818
She's ignoring the principle
of judicial independence by refusing.

484
00:29:11,819 --> 00:29:13,319
Yes, thanks, Coop.

485
00:29:31,379 --> 00:29:35,169
Why didn't you tell me
Marc Thompson was back?

486
00:29:35,259 --> 00:29:37,676
It had nothing to do with me.

487
00:29:38,179 --> 00:29:42,099
- Look, Dad, I have to work with Jo.
- Yeah.

488
00:29:45,659 --> 00:29:47,576
I wanted to marry her.

489
00:29:52,819 --> 00:29:54,069
Is it all over?

490
00:29:56,419 --> 00:29:59,669
Well, she seems very taken
with this man.

491
00:30:01,739 --> 00:30:03,379
What a fool I am.

492
00:30:05,459 --> 00:30:07,793
She won't be happy with him.

493
00:30:08,579 --> 00:30:12,418
Well, she won't tell anybody,
even if she isn't.

494
00:30:12,419 --> 00:30:14,099
You cross with me?

495
00:30:14,619 --> 00:30:16,858
Your carrying on alone
would be very irresponsible.

496
00:30:16,859 --> 00:30:18,058
I might choose not to permit that.

497
00:30:18,059 --> 00:30:19,138
Well, can you stop it?

498
00:30:19,139 --> 00:30:20,778
You'd be surprised
what I can do, Charlie.

499
00:30:20,779 --> 00:30:24,946
Take fresh instructions.
I won't sit until you do.

500
00:30:33,379 --> 00:30:36,818
Well, strictly speaking, Charlie,
he can't stop you leading.

501
00:30:36,819 --> 00:30:41,018
You do have audience in the Crown Court,
in spite of being his daughter.

502
00:30:41,019 --> 00:30:43,338
Could he get appeal
because I'm his daughter?

503
00:30:43,339 --> 00:30:45,338
And who's going to appeal?

504
00:30:45,339 --> 00:30:48,058
- Not your man, if he wins.
- No, the Attorney might.

505
00:30:48,059 --> 00:30:51,738
What? In the case of your beating him?
I doubt it.

506
00:30:51,739 --> 00:30:54,298
Your father is far too inventive
A judge.

507
00:30:54,299 --> 00:30:56,658
He reminds me of Tom Denning.

508
00:30:56,659 --> 00:31:00,858
Although Tom wasn't nearly as good
an advocate as your father.

509
00:31:00,859 --> 00:31:03,538
Would you like me
to have a word with him?

510
00:31:03,539 --> 00:31:06,058
Best not, Grandad.
He'll just say it clouds the issue.

511
00:31:06,059 --> 00:31:07,419
He already does.

512
00:31:07,499 --> 00:31:09,498
But what's the point
of having family connections

513
00:31:09,499 --> 00:31:11,333
if you don't use them?

514
00:31:11,659 --> 00:31:13,138
He's cross with everyone at the moment

515
00:31:13,139 --> 00:31:15,498
because Jo Mills is seeing someone else.

516
00:31:15,499 --> 00:31:17,889
Oh!

517
00:31:18,659 --> 00:31:21,418
That can only improve er
standing as a barrister.

518
00:31:21,419 --> 00:31:24,058
Well, if she could only face Dad.

519
00:31:24,059 --> 00:31:26,978
Look, I'll tell you what, ave
another word with your client.

520
00:31:26,979 --> 00:31:29,978
If he still insists, you must understand
what a grave responsibility

521
00:31:29,979 --> 00:31:31,739
you're taking on.

522
00:31:31,819 --> 00:31:34,298
Clients love you until you lose.

523
00:31:34,299 --> 00:31:35,979
Then the sue you,

524
00:31:36,659 --> 00:31:38,058
and complain to the Bar Council.

525
00:31:38,059 --> 00:31:40,578
It's not a capital offence, Grandad.

526
00:31:40,579 --> 00:31:42,139
Not an more.

527
00:31:43,339 --> 00:31:44,939
Oh, taxi!

528
00:31:56,979 --> 00:31:58,739
All rise.

529
00:32:10,419 --> 00:32:11,779
Mr Norwalk.

530
00:32:12,579 --> 00:32:14,098
My lord, I regret to inform you

531
00:32:14,099 --> 00:32:17,258
that Mr Free has withdrawn
is instructions from me.

532
00:32:17,259 --> 00:32:22,138
As this is a legal aid brief,
it's my duty to inform the court.

533
00:32:22,139 --> 00:32:24,973
Thank you. Mr Free, is this final?

534
00:32:25,819 --> 00:32:28,236
I want Charlie to take over.

535
00:32:33,259 --> 00:32:34,978
Well, this is rather unusual.

536
00:32:34,979 --> 00:32:38,313
Mr Attorney,
do you have an observations?

537
00:32:40,179 --> 00:32:42,778
It must be understood there will be
no allowances made by the Crown

538
00:32:42,779 --> 00:32:44,946
for Miss Deed's juniority.

539
00:32:47,539 --> 00:32:50,058
Well, I'm certainly not going to
make an. Thank you.

540
00:32:50,059 --> 00:32:51,893
Thank you, Mr Norwalk.

541
00:32:51,979 --> 00:32:56,063
- Would your lordship excuse me?
- Yes, of course.

542
00:32:58,339 --> 00:32:59,699
Good luck.

543
00:33:01,899 --> 00:33:05,978
Right. Let's have the jury in.
We've wasted enough time.

544
00:33:05,979 --> 00:33:07,218
Who are you calling first?

545
00:33:07,219 --> 00:33:08,458
Professor Alistair Cluk,

546
00:33:08,459 --> 00:33:11,538
the head of the university
science faculty.

547
00:33:11,539 --> 00:33:14,498
Professor Cluk,
is it possible to quantify

548
00:33:14,499 --> 00:33:18,858
what animal rights protestors have
cost science in this country?

549
00:33:18,859 --> 00:33:20,539
It's incalculable.

550
00:33:20,619 --> 00:33:24,098
Scientists have been held back
by their antics.

551
00:33:24,099 --> 00:33:26,698
Money for experimentation
goes abroad instead.

552
00:33:26,699 --> 00:33:29,338
And the cost to your university?

553
00:33:29,339 --> 00:33:32,298
Added security
and insurance alone run to

554
00:33:32,299 --> 00:33:34,738
hundreds of thousands of pounds a year.

555
00:33:34,739 --> 00:33:37,578
Money that might well have
gone into frontline research?

556
00:33:37,579 --> 00:33:39,458
That's the wickedness of it.

557
00:33:39,459 --> 00:33:41,338
Without their threats and abuse,

558
00:33:41,339 --> 00:33:42,938
we almost certainly
might have found a cure

559
00:33:42,939 --> 00:33:46,298
for the debilitating illnesses
we're still plagued with.

560
00:33:46,299 --> 00:33:48,058
Possibly even for cancer.

561
00:33:48,059 --> 00:33:52,498
- You do still carry out research?
- At great personal cost.

562
00:33:52,499 --> 00:33:55,578
Professor Blackthorn
was plagued by protestors.

563
00:33:55,579 --> 00:33:58,258
It made him so ill,
at times he couldn't work.

564
00:33:58,259 --> 00:34:00,578
What sort of actions occurred?

565
00:34:00,579 --> 00:34:03,498
Dog excrement
on the handles of your cars,

566
00:34:03,499 --> 00:34:07,999
hate mail, abusive phone calls,
threats to your family,

567
00:34:08,019 --> 00:34:13,853
our children and their friends being
stopped and told we are murderers.

568
00:34:17,739 --> 00:34:20,498
- And have you murdered anyone?
- No.

569
00:34:20,499 --> 00:34:23,539
The have sick, warped minds.

570
00:34:23,619 --> 00:34:27,178
Just answer the questions
without the commentary, please.

571
00:34:27,179 --> 00:34:30,938
Clive Blackthorn was vilified
for his work with monkeys

572
00:34:30,939 --> 00:34:34,338
to find a cure for Alzheimer's
and Parkinson's Disease.

573
00:34:34,339 --> 00:34:36,858
Do you know who was targeting him?

574
00:34:36,859 --> 00:34:40,458
The defendant
and his Animal Rights Movement.

575
00:34:40,459 --> 00:34:43,698
They'd push us and jostle us
as we came to work.

576
00:34:43,699 --> 00:34:48,033
Abuse and spit at us,
call us monsters and murderers.

577
00:34:48,339 --> 00:34:50,978
We go on because we believe
what we're doing...

578
00:34:50,979 --> 00:34:53,258
What you're doing is murder!

579
00:34:53,259 --> 00:34:55,676
Mr Free, your turn will come.

580
00:34:56,419 --> 00:34:58,619
And Professor Cluk,

581
00:34:58,699 --> 00:35:02,018
have you had direct contact
with the defendant?

582
00:35:02,019 --> 00:35:05,218
He was at the demonstrations
outside the university.

583
00:35:05,219 --> 00:35:07,938
He and another protestor, David Baxter,

584
00:35:07,939 --> 00:35:11,218
debated the issue with me
at the students' union.

585
00:35:11,219 --> 00:35:13,498
He sent me a lot of abusive emails,

586
00:35:13,499 --> 00:35:15,538
said my wife and children
would be harmed

587
00:35:15,539 --> 00:35:17,578
if I didn't stop abusing animals.

588
00:35:17,579 --> 00:35:20,218
Professor, would you please
stick to the matter at hand?

589
00:35:20,219 --> 00:35:22,553
Well, the police didn't act.

590
00:35:22,779 --> 00:35:24,698
I suppose it was his word against mine.

591
00:35:24,699 --> 00:35:28,116
- So, it's unsubstantiated.
- They're sly.

592
00:35:28,939 --> 00:35:31,738
A new CCTV camera
caught the ringleader, Free,

593
00:35:31,739 --> 00:35:33,978
and the police issued a banning order.

594
00:35:33,979 --> 00:35:37,058
But it didn't stop him getting into
the university and planting that bomb.

595
00:35:37,059 --> 00:35:40,809
Professor, the jury will decide
what happened.

596
00:35:43,099 --> 00:35:46,058
Professor, how much money
as the university spent

597
00:35:46,059 --> 00:35:47,738
on its security system?

598
00:35:47,739 --> 00:35:50,406
Hundreds of thousands of pounds.

599
00:35:50,499 --> 00:35:53,339
Would £400,000 sound about right?

600
00:35:53,419 --> 00:35:56,003
I don't have the exact figure.

601
00:35:56,179 --> 00:35:59,098
It's a very sophisticated,
computer-run system,

602
00:35:59,099 --> 00:36:01,738
with cameras activated by movement,
I believe.

603
00:36:01,739 --> 00:36:03,179
Yes, it is.

604
00:36:03,259 --> 00:36:06,418
It takes an inordinate amount
of your precious funds.

605
00:36:06,419 --> 00:36:09,938
Yet, nowhere in the whole system
are there any images

606
00:36:09,939 --> 00:36:14,439
of Mr Free entering the building
and planting the bomb.

607
00:36:15,659 --> 00:36:17,059
Why is that?

608
00:36:17,579 --> 00:36:20,418
There are images of him caught on camera
in the building.

609
00:36:20,419 --> 00:36:24,978
- Around the time the bomb was planted?
- He could have planted it any time.

610
00:36:24,979 --> 00:36:29,498
Professor, you called the bombed
building an animal hotel.

611
00:36:29,499 --> 00:36:32,938
The animals would have lived
in five-star luxury.

612
00:36:32,939 --> 00:36:36,658
Warmth, light, play, food,
until the were bombed.

613
00:36:36,659 --> 00:36:41,178
Fact is, caged animals, no
matter ow comfortable the cages,

614
00:36:41,179 --> 00:36:44,578
live in terror of humans,
of the bolts going back,

615
00:36:44,579 --> 00:36:48,418
fearing that the will be the next
for some horrible experiment.

616
00:36:48,419 --> 00:36:51,098
- Is that a question?
- Romantic nonsense.

617
00:36:51,099 --> 00:36:52,938
Animals don't have
that sort of awareness.

618
00:36:52,939 --> 00:36:56,058
- Then why do you use them?
- The are treated with respect.

619
00:36:56,059 --> 00:36:59,538
Sewing up their eyes?
Implanting electrodes in their brains?

620
00:36:59,539 --> 00:37:02,218
Infecting them with syphilis, with AIDS?

621
00:37:02,219 --> 00:37:03,658
To find answers for humans.

622
00:37:03,659 --> 00:37:07,298
But these experiments on monkeys
bring no benefits to humans.

623
00:37:07,299 --> 00:37:09,258
- This has no bearing.
- I agree.

624
00:37:09,259 --> 00:37:11,378
- Of course the do.
- No cure has resulted.

625
00:37:11,379 --> 00:37:13,938
- We're getting closer.
- More people are ding of cancer

626
00:37:13,939 --> 00:37:17,138
after millions of animals are used
in cancer research.

627
00:37:17,139 --> 00:37:18,898
- My lord.
- Anti-rejection drugs

628
00:37:18,899 --> 00:37:21,458
responsible for kidney damage
in human beings

629
00:37:21,459 --> 00:37:24,378
aren't toxic for dogs,
yet still they're tested on them.

630
00:37:24,379 --> 00:37:26,298
- My lord, what is this?
- Miss Deed.

631
00:37:26,299 --> 00:37:29,378
- We're finding a cure for AIDS.
- Monkeys don't die of AIDS.

632
00:37:29,379 --> 00:37:31,698
- Miss Deed, stop.
- We can't use humans!

633
00:37:31,699 --> 00:37:33,778
Why? They're the only true model!

634
00:37:33,779 --> 00:37:35,138
- We use animals.
- My lord.

635
00:37:35,139 --> 00:37:37,538
- Miss Deed.
- You are addicted to cruelty.

636
00:37:37,539 --> 00:37:41,456
Animals will provide a cure!
They will! Damn you!

637
00:37:41,459 --> 00:37:42,819
And him!

638
00:37:42,899 --> 00:37:45,018
My lord, this is
the most disgraceful behaviour.

639
00:37:45,019 --> 00:37:47,378
Yes. Miss Deed,
I'll see you in chambers.

640
00:37:47,379 --> 00:37:49,339
All rise.

641
00:37:55,379 --> 00:37:57,818
The Attorney General is right.
Your behaviour was deplorable.

642
00:37:57,819 --> 00:37:59,698
The witness was arrogant
and self-righteous.

643
00:37:59,699 --> 00:38:02,098
If Sir Alan doesn't report you
to the Bar Council,

644
00:38:02,099 --> 00:38:03,299
I will.

645
00:38:03,819 --> 00:38:06,986
- I was defending my client.
- Enough!

646
00:38:07,499 --> 00:38:08,729
My lord.

647
00:38:10,259 --> 00:38:12,778
Your line in cross-examination
is foolish.

648
00:38:12,779 --> 00:38:15,618
You make it look as if you're trying to
defend the attack on the laboratories.

649
00:38:15,619 --> 00:38:17,618
I'm not. He didn't do it.

650
00:38:17,619 --> 00:38:19,898
Get yourself sorted out
in relation to your client

651
00:38:19,899 --> 00:38:24,018
or your career will go nowhere.
And you will apologise to Sir Alan.

652
00:38:24,019 --> 00:38:27,859
- I didn't do anything.
- Not another word!

653
00:38:33,739 --> 00:38:36,823
- Thank you, Sonia.
- Pleasure, Judge.

654
00:38:40,499 --> 00:38:45,083
- It's not a laughing matter.
- Oh, I know that, Judge.

655
00:38:46,059 --> 00:38:47,778
You should try and get
the charge changed.

656
00:38:47,779 --> 00:38:50,738
You can't, Charlie.
Anyway, it will just anger your dad.

657
00:38:50,739 --> 00:38:54,073
No. No, a lesser charge
is a good tactic.

658
00:38:54,099 --> 00:38:55,433
Try it, Charlie.

659
00:38:56,659 --> 00:38:57,993
All rise.

660
00:39:13,219 --> 00:39:14,579
Miss Deed.

661
00:39:16,179 --> 00:39:18,858
I would like to apologise to the court
and to the Attorney General

662
00:39:18,859 --> 00:39:20,419
for my conduct.

663
00:39:20,499 --> 00:39:22,416
I accept your apology.

664
00:39:24,579 --> 00:39:28,618
Ma I address the court
in the absence of the jury, my lord?

665
00:39:28,619 --> 00:39:29,619
Yes.

666
00:39:30,339 --> 00:39:32,898
Doubtless
this is a complex piece of law,

667
00:39:32,899 --> 00:39:35,566
so rather than bore you with it,

668
00:39:35,739 --> 00:39:40,823
why don't you take a break,
get yourselves some tea or coffee?

669
00:39:50,539 --> 00:39:51,659
Yes.

670
00:39:53,459 --> 00:39:56,658
My lord, the prosecution suggested
that my client plead guilt

671
00:39:56,659 --> 00:39:58,019
to manslaughter.

672
00:39:58,099 --> 00:40:00,058
My lord, no such offer was made.

673
00:40:00,059 --> 00:40:02,018
Miss Deed, you shouldn't be
telling me this.

674
00:40:02,019 --> 00:40:05,138
It's only fair that the charge be re-put
as manslaughter resulting from...

675
00:40:05,139 --> 00:40:06,978
No, it's a question of
whether your client

676
00:40:06,979 --> 00:40:10,378
unlawfully killed the deceased
and with what intention.

677
00:40:10,379 --> 00:40:14,138
In these circumstances, manslaughter
is always an alternative to murder.

678
00:40:14,139 --> 00:40:18,818
Your academic knowledge of the law
ought to be very fresh.

679
00:40:18,819 --> 00:40:20,978
With respect, my lord,
I was going on to say

680
00:40:20,979 --> 00:40:23,229
aggravated criminal damage.

681
00:40:25,139 --> 00:40:26,806
Oh, I see.

682
00:40:26,858 --> 00:40:30,458
- Is that an option, Mr Attorney?
- Well, hardly, my lord.

683
00:40:30,459 --> 00:40:33,378
Might I ask the courtes to have a word
with those who instruct me?

684
00:40:33,379 --> 00:40:34,449
Yes.

685
00:40:52,259 --> 00:40:55,338
My lord, in view of the way
the trial has been conducted,

686
00:40:55,339 --> 00:40:57,738
we respectfully request that only murder

687
00:40:57,739 --> 00:40:59,458
be considered on the indictment.

688
00:40:59,459 --> 00:41:01,059
All or nothing.

689
00:41:01,139 --> 00:41:03,306
That's raising the stakes.

690
00:41:04,619 --> 00:41:06,938
I will hear you both
at the end of the trial

691
00:41:06,939 --> 00:41:09,458
as to what alternatives, if any,

692
00:41:09,459 --> 00:41:11,338
- I leave to the jury.
- Of course, my lord.

693
00:41:11,339 --> 00:41:13,938
Good. Now, do you understand
what that means, Mr Free?

694
00:41:13,939 --> 00:41:17,458
At the end of the da, I will decide,
having listened to the barristers,

695
00:41:17,459 --> 00:41:20,058
whether murder alone
is for the jury to consider.

696
00:41:20,059 --> 00:41:22,309
Do you understand that, yes?

697
00:41:23,139 --> 00:41:26,618
All right.
In view of the hour, I'm going to rise.

698
00:41:26,619 --> 00:41:29,818
Miss Deed, this will give you time
to consult your client

699
00:41:29,819 --> 00:41:32,418
before he's whisked away
by the prison van.

700
00:41:32,419 --> 00:41:36,298
Usher, release the jury,
with my apologies, until tomorrow.

701
00:41:36,299 --> 00:41:37,633
All rise.

702
00:41:38,379 --> 00:41:40,058
This is really serious now, Henry.

703
00:41:40,059 --> 00:41:42,698
- Nothing's changed, Charlie.
- Henry.

704
00:41:42,699 --> 00:41:45,178
You can get a whole life sentence.

705
00:41:45,179 --> 00:41:46,898
If the find me guilt.

706
00:41:46,899 --> 00:41:48,858
What if the secret witness
puts you there?

707
00:41:48,859 --> 00:41:50,298
How could he? I wasn't.

708
00:41:50,299 --> 00:41:54,133
Yeah, he could lie.
Did you ever consider that?

709
00:41:54,499 --> 00:41:55,658
You don't need me, Charlie.

710
00:41:55,659 --> 00:41:58,938
Given how the Attorney responded
after you had taken his witness apart,

711
00:41:58,939 --> 00:42:00,098
I'd say you're doing rather well.

712
00:42:00,099 --> 00:42:02,818
Do you think dad's really going to
report me to the Bar Council?

713
00:42:02,819 --> 00:42:03,898
It'd be out of character.

714
00:42:03,899 --> 00:42:08,778
Well, he's furious because he thinks
I knew about you and Marc.

715
00:42:08,779 --> 00:42:10,579
Is that what he said?

716
00:42:10,659 --> 00:42:14,418
He only has himself to blame.
I mean, what does he think women are?

717
00:42:14,419 --> 00:42:17,858
He'll be really ticked off
if he thinks I'm helping you.

718
00:42:17,859 --> 00:42:20,538
- Please, Jo.
- Have another go at the PII.

719
00:42:20,539 --> 00:42:24,178
It is so unfair that the defendant's
accuser should have anonymity.

720
00:42:24,179 --> 00:42:25,378
Simon Norwalk went along with it.

721
00:42:25,379 --> 00:42:26,778
Well, there's no reason you should.

722
00:42:26,779 --> 00:42:30,498
- Speak to the judge without the jury.
- I haven't identified the law et.

723
00:42:30,499 --> 00:42:33,538
Ex parte Bennett number 2, 1994,
I think.

724
00:42:33,539 --> 00:42:36,178
The case is cited by
Lord Justice Simon Brown.

725
00:42:36,179 --> 00:42:39,379
So, I said to my client,

726
00:42:39,459 --> 00:42:42,543
"Look, now you are finally acquitted,

727
00:42:42,739 --> 00:42:45,659
"tell me truly, did you do it?"

728
00:42:45,739 --> 00:42:47,818
And he turned to me and he said,

729
00:42:47,819 --> 00:42:50,338
"Well, guv, after listening to
your amazing argument,

730
00:42:50,339 --> 00:42:52,978
"I'm beginning to think I didn't."

731
00:42:52,979 --> 00:42:55,418
We spent all this money on legal aid

732
00:42:55,419 --> 00:42:59,458
and then little irks like Free
sack the barrister.

733
00:42:59,459 --> 00:43:01,978
Well, Deed will have to report Charlie
to the Bar Council.

734
00:43:01,979 --> 00:43:04,896
- Oh, I wouldn't count on it.
- Oh?

735
00:43:06,139 --> 00:43:11,418
George, you look as if you spent
a fearsome amount on that dress.

736
00:43:11,419 --> 00:43:13,938
I didn't find it in Oxfam.

737
00:43:13,939 --> 00:43:17,898
Could have solved Third World debt
with that dress.

738
00:43:17,899 --> 00:43:20,399
I need to speak to you alone.

739
00:43:21,099 --> 00:43:23,099
- Excuse us.
- Of course.

740
00:43:23,339 --> 00:43:27,218
This have anything to do with
darling Jo leaving you?

741
00:43:27,219 --> 00:43:29,298
My, my. Isn't Charlie a chatterbox?

742
00:43:29,299 --> 00:43:34,216
She is my daughter, John.
In fact, we've become quite close.

743
00:43:34,299 --> 00:43:36,966
I came here to ask your advice.

744
00:43:38,219 --> 00:43:42,338
If it's about Little Miss Oxfam,
she's done you a big favour.

745
00:43:42,339 --> 00:43:43,779
Keep walking.

746
00:43:45,099 --> 00:43:47,849
Charlie's in trouble in my court.

747
00:43:48,339 --> 00:43:50,423
Will you put her in jail?

748
00:43:51,099 --> 00:43:52,978
Have you been helping her?

749
00:43:52,979 --> 00:43:56,563
Daddy's trying to persuade me
to help.

750
00:43:56,978 --> 00:43:58,378
Crime isn't my speciality.

751
00:43:58,379 --> 00:44:03,378
No, no, it's not that.
It's not law. I can steer her on that.

752
00:44:03,379 --> 00:44:07,218
I think she's too emotionally involved
with her client's cause.

753
00:44:07,219 --> 00:44:08,698
Well, you might have to slap her down.

754
00:44:08,699 --> 00:44:11,618
Well, the appeal court
might do that to me, anyway.

755
00:44:11,619 --> 00:44:13,978
The don't like children
appearing before parent.

756
00:44:13,979 --> 00:44:18,979
Oh! Hmm.
What about favouring lovers?

757
00:44:20,939 --> 00:44:22,689
I never did, George.

758
00:44:23,899 --> 00:44:29,233
It would be very interesting
to see Jo Mills in front of you now.

759
00:44:30,219 --> 00:44:32,049
Your cruel streak,

760
00:44:32,739 --> 00:44:34,219
so attractive.

761
00:44:36,299 --> 00:44:37,339
Oh.

762
00:44:38,379 --> 00:44:42,379
Looks like the guest of honour
would like a word.

763
00:44:44,139 --> 00:44:45,898
Do you know George Channing?

764
00:44:45,899 --> 00:44:49,418
- You're Joe's daughter?
- So I'm told.

765
00:44:49,419 --> 00:44:52,018
- Do you mind if I take John away?
- Oh, by all means.

766
00:44:52,019 --> 00:44:55,519
- He needs a holiday.
- That would be nice.

767
00:44:56,059 --> 00:44:58,178
- The chief likes your ideas for prisons.
- Oh, good.

768
00:44:58,179 --> 00:45:00,858
He wondered if you'd be interested
in running a judicial inquiry?

769
00:45:00,859 --> 00:45:05,729
My lords, ladies and gentlemen,
dinner is now served.

770
00:45:05,819 --> 00:45:08,986
Good, I'm hungry. Shall we go in?

771
00:45:09,419 --> 00:45:11,618
- Did you hear that?
- It will happen, George.

772
00:45:11,619 --> 00:45:14,338
We need more prisons,
not more empt prisons.

773
00:45:14,339 --> 00:45:16,858
I thought Deed was heartbroken
about Jo Mills.

774
00:45:16,859 --> 00:45:20,139
Oh! John's heart heals fast.

775
00:45:20,219 --> 00:45:23,458
He obviously doesn't realise
he's sleeping with the enemy.

776
00:45:23,459 --> 00:45:25,709
She is Ian Rochester's man.

777
00:45:28,699 --> 00:45:32,538
Are you sure you have enough law
to carry this argument?

778
00:45:32,539 --> 00:45:33,899
I think so.

779
00:45:34,459 --> 00:45:36,338
I'm not sure
why I'm doing this, Charlie.

780
00:45:36,339 --> 00:45:39,258
Neil certainly doesn't want
Henry Free to win.

781
00:45:39,259 --> 00:45:41,978
Well, Henry shouldn't go to prison
for something he didn't do.

782
00:45:41,979 --> 00:45:46,813
There are plenty of things
Mr Free should go to prison for.

783
00:45:47,539 --> 00:45:51,123
- I don't get you sometimes, Mum.
- Well...

784
00:45:51,259 --> 00:45:53,593
blood is thicker than water.

785
00:45:53,699 --> 00:45:55,458
My lord, my learned friend
the Attorney General

786
00:45:55,459 --> 00:45:58,058
has been called away
on urgent government business.

787
00:45:58,059 --> 00:45:59,099
Oh.

788
00:46:03,019 --> 00:46:04,298
I would like to address the court

789
00:46:04,299 --> 00:46:06,898
on the matter of
Public Interest Immunity

790
00:46:06,899 --> 00:46:09,258
in relationship to
the prosecution's main witness.

791
00:46:09,259 --> 00:46:10,698
My lord, this matter as been dealt with.

792
00:46:10,699 --> 00:46:14,098
Yes, Miss Deed, earlier counsel
for the defence accepted my ruling.

793
00:46:14,099 --> 00:46:17,698
With respect, my lord, Mr Norwalk
is no longer running this case.

794
00:46:17,699 --> 00:46:20,738
My duties to my client
could not be properly executed

795
00:46:20,739 --> 00:46:23,538
were I not allowed
to argue this point afresh.

796
00:46:23,539 --> 00:46:24,858
Well, accepting that this

797
00:46:24,859 --> 00:46:27,218
could be looked at again,
what is your point?

798
00:46:27,219 --> 00:46:30,553
My lord,
this matter has been dealt with.

799
00:46:30,739 --> 00:46:32,138
This is not a civil case,

800
00:46:32,139 --> 00:46:35,618
where someone is suing the police
for the identity of an informant.

801
00:46:35,619 --> 00:46:38,953
This man's liberty for life is at stake.

802
00:46:39,659 --> 00:46:42,258
In The Queen v. Horseferry Road
Magistrates Court,

803
00:46:42,259 --> 00:46:44,338
Ex parte Bennett number 2,

804
00:46:44,339 --> 00:46:46,898
Lord Justice Simon Brown states,

805
00:46:46,899 --> 00:46:50,058
"It is more important
that an innocent man be acquitted

806
00:46:50,059 --> 00:46:52,578
"than a guilt one be convicted."

807
00:46:52,579 --> 00:46:55,809
Lord Justice Bingham,
as he then was,

808
00:46:55,899 --> 00:46:59,978
that even the name of the informant
ma be revealed if it's necessary

809
00:46:59,979 --> 00:47:02,738
in establishing
the defendant's innocence.

810
00:47:02,739 --> 00:47:05,658
If you can demonstrate that your client

811
00:47:05,659 --> 00:47:08,378
can't get a fair trial
without revealing the name,

812
00:47:08,379 --> 00:47:10,179
then we'll reveal it.

813
00:47:13,099 --> 00:47:15,418
How do we know that the
person hiding behind PII

814
00:47:15,419 --> 00:47:17,538
doesn't have an agenda of his own?

815
00:47:17,539 --> 00:47:21,873
Well, you're at liberty
to cross-examine the witness.

816
00:47:22,619 --> 00:47:24,536
This is grossly unfair.

817
00:47:27,179 --> 00:47:30,263
Is there something you'd like to add?

818
00:47:31,299 --> 00:47:32,699
No, my lord.

819
00:47:33,579 --> 00:47:34,899
Sir Peter?

820
00:47:35,139 --> 00:47:36,458
I would like next to call the witness

821
00:47:36,459 --> 00:47:38,338
whose identity is
to be protected, my lord.

822
00:47:38,339 --> 00:47:41,378
Ah yes, this witness
is to be present in court.

823
00:47:41,379 --> 00:47:43,058
We'll use the screens, Mr Harrison.

824
00:47:43,059 --> 00:47:45,778
Would you prefer the jury
in place first, my lord?

825
00:47:45,779 --> 00:47:48,363
Yes, I want the jury in place.

826
00:47:48,979 --> 00:47:50,313
To you.

827
00:47:50,699 --> 00:47:51,979
No, no. To me.

828
00:47:52,579 --> 00:47:54,859
In... that thing.

829
00:48:01,179 --> 00:48:02,538
Members of the jury,

830
00:48:02,539 --> 00:48:05,458
you must attach no prejudice
to the defendant

831
00:48:05,459 --> 00:48:07,098
because of this procedure.

832
00:48:07,099 --> 00:48:11,098
It's simply a matter of protecting
the witness's identity.

833
00:48:11,099 --> 00:48:15,599
And for this reason, is voice
also has been disguised.

834
00:48:15,659 --> 00:48:17,489
All right? Sir Peter.

835
00:48:18,579 --> 00:48:22,419
Mr X, are you a British national?

836
00:48:22,499 --> 00:48:24,259
Yes, I am, sir.

837
00:48:24,339 --> 00:48:27,938
- Do you live and work in this country?
- Yes, I do.

838
00:48:27,939 --> 00:48:31,098
- Do you know the defendant?
- Yes, I do.

839
00:48:31,099 --> 00:48:33,516
Tell the court where you met.

840
00:48:34,019 --> 00:48:36,218
At a Cambridge University protest

841
00:48:36,219 --> 00:48:40,298
to stop the experimental labs
that house monkeys from being built.

842
00:48:40,299 --> 00:48:42,883
- Did that succeed?
- Yes.

843
00:48:43,139 --> 00:48:45,498
The university backed out of it
when the council turned down

844
00:48:45,499 --> 00:48:46,938
their planning application.

845
00:48:46,939 --> 00:48:50,439
How did the protest succeed
in stopping it?

846
00:48:51,419 --> 00:48:53,058
Protestors intimidated everyone,

847
00:48:53,059 --> 00:48:56,098
from the university
to the builders' suppliers.

848
00:48:56,099 --> 00:48:58,619
Was this legitimate protest?

849
00:49:00,219 --> 00:49:03,498
The law wasn't in place to stop it.
That's why I was sent in.

850
00:49:03,499 --> 00:49:06,179
So, it was legitimate?

851
00:49:10,299 --> 00:49:11,819
Sir Peter.

852
00:49:11,899 --> 00:49:14,066
What precisely did you do?

853
00:49:14,819 --> 00:49:19,538
I infiltrated the movement
and I got close to its leaders.

854
00:49:19,539 --> 00:49:23,789
I was leafleting on the streets,
going to protests,

855
00:49:24,819 --> 00:49:26,258
doing things that got me noticed.

856
00:49:26,259 --> 00:49:29,009
Were there an illegal activities?

857
00:49:29,779 --> 00:49:34,196
- Some were, sir.
- Well, don't tell us what the were.

858
00:49:34,659 --> 00:49:38,493
How long did it take you
to infiltrate the ARM?

859
00:49:39,179 --> 00:49:41,059
It took over a year.

860
00:49:41,139 --> 00:49:43,378
Did you meet the defendant?

861
00:49:43,379 --> 00:49:45,018
I met him frequently.

862
00:49:45,019 --> 00:49:47,498
He was all over the organisation,
the linchpin,

863
00:49:47,499 --> 00:49:48,578
that's why we targeted him.

864
00:49:48,579 --> 00:49:51,578
Were you able to get information
from the centre?

865
00:49:51,579 --> 00:49:53,059
Loads of stuff.

866
00:49:53,139 --> 00:49:58,473
The defendant was sharing information,
some of it highly illegal.

867
00:49:58,579 --> 00:50:01,496
He said we should act like Al-Qaida

868
00:50:02,459 --> 00:50:04,498
and take down some buildings.

869
00:50:04,499 --> 00:50:05,939
That's a lie.

870
00:50:06,619 --> 00:50:11,818
Did he specifically mention
the bombing of the animal hotel?

871
00:50:11,819 --> 00:50:13,653
His number one target.

872
00:50:21,539 --> 00:50:26,218
If you knew it was going to happen,
why didn't you stop it?

873
00:50:26,219 --> 00:50:28,136
I'm not Superman.

874
00:50:28,339 --> 00:50:31,098
The truth is,
you weren't interested in stopping it.

875
00:50:31,099 --> 00:50:32,178
That's stupid.

876
00:50:32,179 --> 00:50:36,658
Explosions are far more damning
than something that might happen.

877
00:50:36,659 --> 00:50:40,159
I might have stopped
Clive being murdered.

878
00:50:40,259 --> 00:50:42,676
- Professor Blackthorn?
- Yes.

879
00:50:43,579 --> 00:50:45,663
Was he a friend?

880
00:50:47,819 --> 00:50:49,819
Yes. We met a few times.

881
00:50:50,299 --> 00:50:52,883
In what circumstances?

882
00:50:53,659 --> 00:50:57,658
I got information from him
about threats he'd received.

883
00:50:57,659 --> 00:51:01,938
- Can you tell us your name?
- Miss Deed, that is not permitted.

884
00:51:01,939 --> 00:51:04,138
- Then tell us who you work for.
- My lord.

885
00:51:04,139 --> 00:51:06,306
Miss Deed, don't go there.

886
00:51:07,939 --> 00:51:09,738
The fact is, Mr Coward...

887
00:51:09,739 --> 00:51:11,738
Don't call him "Mr Coward".

888
00:51:11,739 --> 00:51:15,898
Well, hiding behind your screen
to make your accusations,

889
00:51:15,899 --> 00:51:18,399
you don't know Mr Free at all.

890
00:51:20,059 --> 00:51:23,258
He led a raid on Tri-Green Breeding Farm

891
00:51:23,259 --> 00:51:26,698
and released a thousand mice
and 40 breeding beagles.

892
00:51:26,699 --> 00:51:28,299
Ask your client.

893
00:51:30,899 --> 00:51:33,418
You're a coward and a liar
who gets excited

894
00:51:33,419 --> 00:51:35,418
by the power that informing gives you.

895
00:51:35,419 --> 00:51:38,778
Miss Deed, are you going
to hurl accusations

896
00:51:38,779 --> 00:51:40,419
or ask questions?

897
00:51:43,259 --> 00:51:44,458
When do you claim the defendant

898
00:51:44,459 --> 00:51:47,938
first mentioned
the bombing of the university?

899
00:51:47,939 --> 00:51:50,218
I'm not sure.
He said it a number of times.

900
00:51:50,219 --> 00:51:55,178
You're spying on people who want to stop
the torture and suffering of animals.

901
00:51:55,179 --> 00:51:57,846
Miss Deed, stop making speeches.

902
00:51:58,819 --> 00:52:02,258
A police spy hears
some alarming statements,

903
00:52:02,259 --> 00:52:06,138
yet doesn't take key details
of these incriminating statements.

904
00:52:06,139 --> 00:52:09,306
Is that what you're telling this jury?

905
00:52:11,059 --> 00:52:13,393
We all made such statements.

906
00:52:14,059 --> 00:52:16,418
So, you're an agent provocateur.

907
00:52:16,419 --> 00:52:17,498
Of course I'm not.

908
00:52:17,499 --> 00:52:19,999
You suggested planting a bomb?

909
00:52:20,179 --> 00:52:23,263
No, I joined in to keep their trust.

910
00:52:23,659 --> 00:52:26,258
Did you plant the bomb
to keep their trust?

911
00:52:26,259 --> 00:52:29,176
- Of course I didn't.
- Really?

912
00:52:29,699 --> 00:52:31,578
I'm not sure the jury believes you.

913
00:52:31,579 --> 00:52:35,829
That's something we'll all
ave to wait to discover.

914
00:52:37,099 --> 00:52:40,599
My lord, might this be
a convenient moment?

915
00:52:42,739 --> 00:52:44,099
Yes...

916
00:52:44,859 --> 00:52:47,658
I think this might be a suitable moment.

917
00:52:47,659 --> 00:52:50,578
Members of the jury,
I must remind you not to discuss this

918
00:52:50,579 --> 00:52:52,913
unless you are all together.

919
00:52:53,099 --> 00:52:54,579
Till the morning.

920
00:52:55,059 --> 00:52:56,659
All rise.

921
00:53:01,739 --> 00:53:04,258
- That was brilliant, Charlie.
- Think it was all right?

922
00:53:04,259 --> 00:53:06,426
It was great. I love you.

923
00:53:07,499 --> 00:53:09,999
You're not allowed to do that.

924
00:53:11,739 --> 00:53:14,538
Let's hope his name comes out
or we're not much further forward.

925
00:53:14,539 --> 00:53:17,938
- Jonathan's is right.
- I promise you, he's not one of us.

926
00:53:17,939 --> 00:53:21,018
What he's saying
must give some clue who he is.

927
00:53:21,019 --> 00:53:23,898
- But Henry didn't say an of that.
- Well, he's made it all up.

928
00:53:23,899 --> 00:53:28,218
I can't pretend I didn't want that place
or all the other horror shows destroyed.

929
00:53:28,219 --> 00:53:29,969
But not at that cost.

930
00:53:29,979 --> 00:53:33,698
I only hate what the vivisectors do.
I don't hate them.

931
00:53:33,699 --> 00:53:35,499
Who didn't you trust?

932
00:53:36,219 --> 00:53:38,778
I trusted everyone close to me.

933
00:53:38,779 --> 00:53:41,178
Just go on attacking him, Charlie.

934
00:53:41,179 --> 00:53:43,263
If the judge will let me.

935
00:53:43,899 --> 00:53:48,649
Did anyone ever raise the idea
about bombing the building?

936
00:53:49,219 --> 00:53:51,778
We all say things when we're angry.

937
00:53:51,779 --> 00:53:56,779
How we'd like to kill the vivisectors,
infect them with AIDS,

938
00:53:56,819 --> 00:53:59,698
sew their eyes up
like the do to monkeys.

939
00:53:59,699 --> 00:54:03,258
Yeah, but did anybody in the group
ever talk about bombing the building?

940
00:54:03,259 --> 00:54:07,058
- We all say things in anger.
- Could anyone have carried it through?

941
00:54:07,059 --> 00:54:08,578
No one's that stupid.

942
00:54:08,579 --> 00:54:12,378
- People do get frustrated, Henry.
- We care about animals, Charlie.

943
00:54:12,379 --> 00:54:17,098
- Humans are animals, too.
- Fact is, someone bombed the building.

944
00:54:17,099 --> 00:54:20,058
If only you'd let us use your alibi.

945
00:54:20,059 --> 00:54:21,459
No, Charlie.

946
00:54:21,939 --> 00:54:24,578
Then the next best thing
is to suggest someone else did it.

947
00:54:24,579 --> 00:54:26,578
Not someone from the movement.

948
00:54:26,579 --> 00:54:29,996
Right now,
all I'm concerned about is you.

949
00:54:31,339 --> 00:54:34,898
Look, give me the names
of everyone else involved.

950
00:54:34,899 --> 00:54:39,138
I'll try to identify the informant
through process of elimination.

951
00:54:39,139 --> 00:54:40,779
I can't, Charlie.

952
00:54:42,019 --> 00:54:43,779
Don't you trust me?

953
00:54:45,099 --> 00:54:49,433
These people would die
before the betray the animals.

954
00:54:51,699 --> 00:54:54,199
I'd sooner go to jail.

955
00:54:56,419 --> 00:54:57,699
For life?

956
00:55:02,339 --> 00:55:05,098
It's hopeless, Jo.
He won't let me use his alibi.

957
00:55:05,099 --> 00:55:06,539
Hmm.

958
00:55:06,619 --> 00:55:10,018
Perhaps Simon Norwalk had a better sense
of your client than he realised.

959
00:55:10,019 --> 00:55:12,138
Maybe that's why he suggested a deal.

960
00:55:12,139 --> 00:55:14,458
Henry didn't bomb that building.

961
00:55:14,459 --> 00:55:18,293
- Then he has to help you prove it.
- He won't.

962
00:55:19,419 --> 00:55:22,738
He was rescuing animals
from a lab in Bedford.

963
00:55:22,739 --> 00:55:23,809
Ah!

964
00:55:24,139 --> 00:55:27,098
Isn't there an way
that I can learn Mr X's identity?

965
00:55:27,099 --> 00:55:30,418
Be careful, Charlie. His identity
is protected, even from you.

966
00:55:30,419 --> 00:55:32,669
This is Henry's whole life.

967
00:55:32,739 --> 00:55:34,858
Are you becoming emotionally attached
to this client?

968
00:55:34,859 --> 00:55:35,929
Yes!

969
00:55:37,499 --> 00:55:38,569
No.

970
00:55:39,419 --> 00:55:40,538
No, of course not.

971
00:55:40,539 --> 00:55:42,658
You wouldn't be the first barrister
to have done so.

972
00:55:42,659 --> 00:55:44,419
I'm not that stupid.

973
00:55:46,739 --> 00:55:49,258
Maybe I should talk to his friends
that we're calling as witnesses.

974
00:55:49,259 --> 00:55:51,898
There's a risk.
You could be accused of fabrication.

975
00:55:51,899 --> 00:55:54,538
The whole system
seems weighted against him

976
00:55:54,539 --> 00:55:56,956
and, well, Dad's not helping.

977
00:55:57,619 --> 00:55:58,849
Charlie.

978
00:56:02,819 --> 00:56:05,569
I've got a free morning tomorrow.

979
00:56:09,539 --> 00:56:12,258
You can't talk to the witnesses.
It's not allowed.

980
00:56:12,259 --> 00:56:14,738
I'm just trying to identify
who this infiltrator is.

981
00:56:14,739 --> 00:56:16,989
Will it make an difference?

982
00:56:18,539 --> 00:56:24,039
Whoever it is has to be the police
or MI5 or someone close to them.

983
00:56:27,179 --> 00:56:29,139
Who was close to Henry?

984
00:56:37,739 --> 00:56:41,419
Be careful. We're calling some of these.

985
00:56:42,339 --> 00:56:44,589
Great. I best get started.

986
00:56:44,979 --> 00:56:47,063
Can we go for that drink?

987
00:56:48,459 --> 00:56:50,219
I'll call you later.

988
00:56:59,099 --> 00:57:00,818
Were you with Henry for his alibi?

989
00:57:00,819 --> 00:57:02,498
Would you like to join your demo?

990
00:57:02,499 --> 00:57:04,458
Look, I'm trying to help him.
Why wouldn't you want that?

991
00:57:04,459 --> 00:57:06,179
He knows the risks.

992
00:57:07,779 --> 00:57:10,818
- Who are you talking to?
- No one. No one will talk to me.

993
00:57:10,819 --> 00:57:13,903
Have you tried Phil Adams or Alan Day?

994
00:57:17,099 --> 00:57:19,849
David Baxter's not there, either.

995
00:57:22,299 --> 00:57:23,378
Who gave you my name?

996
00:57:23,379 --> 00:57:25,738
I'm just trying to identify
who informed on Henry.

997
00:57:25,739 --> 00:57:26,818
Well, it wasn't me.

998
00:57:26,819 --> 00:57:28,098
Well, it had to be someone
from the movement.

999
00:57:28,099 --> 00:57:30,516
What? You must be kidding me.

1000
00:57:30,859 --> 00:57:32,219
How's that?

1001
00:57:33,939 --> 00:57:37,773
- Who do you suspect?
- Does anyone hate Henry?

1002
00:57:39,859 --> 00:57:42,193
Only those who hurt animals.

1003
00:57:44,339 --> 00:57:46,498
Why would anyone bother
killing Professor Blackthorn?

1004
00:57:46,499 --> 00:57:48,459
He was ding of cancer.

1005
00:57:48,779 --> 00:57:49,979
Was he?

1006
00:57:53,059 --> 00:57:54,379
All right.

1007
00:57:56,339 --> 00:57:57,939
All right, look.

1008
00:57:58,579 --> 00:58:02,413
It would be good if you can find out
who it is.

1009
00:58:02,499 --> 00:58:07,058
I mean, what names have you got?
Maybe I can give you some.

1010
00:58:07,059 --> 00:58:08,699
Come on. Get in.

1011
00:58:11,859 --> 00:58:14,258
- Do you want to come in?
- No.

1012
00:58:14,259 --> 00:58:16,343
No. I like watching you.

1013
00:58:19,579 --> 00:58:23,098
- Am I doing the right thing?
- What, being with me?

1014
00:58:23,099 --> 00:58:25,898
Helping Charlie in court tomorrow.

1015
00:58:25,899 --> 00:58:28,978
I'm worried, in case
I make things worse for her.

1016
00:58:28,979 --> 00:58:32,618
I thought judges were supposed
to rise above such petty emotions.

1017
00:58:32,619 --> 00:58:33,619
In theory.

1018
00:58:35,499 --> 00:58:37,179
Is this about us?

1019
00:58:40,739 --> 00:58:45,099
Does it really matter
what he thinks an more?

1020
00:58:45,179 --> 00:58:46,249
Hmm?

1021
00:58:49,419 --> 00:58:50,978
What sort of mood is he in?

1022
00:58:50,979 --> 00:58:52,178
Not good, Charlie.

1023
00:58:52,179 --> 00:58:54,338
Sir Peter Dulford was in to see him.

1024
00:58:54,339 --> 00:58:57,839
- What about?
- Best let him tell you that.

1025
00:58:59,579 --> 00:59:03,246
- You couldn't have been more stupid.
- What?

1026
00:59:04,059 --> 00:59:06,618
Charlie, that's a tone you might have
got away with when you were five.

1027
00:59:06,619 --> 00:59:07,849
Not now.

1028
00:59:09,019 --> 00:59:11,978
Sir Peter has complained that
you have been interviewing witnesses.

1029
00:59:11,979 --> 00:59:14,138
Trying to discover the identity
of the informant.

1030
00:59:14,139 --> 00:59:15,779
How does he know?

1031
00:59:16,579 --> 00:59:19,058
Well, I hoped you might den it.

1032
00:59:19,059 --> 00:59:20,539
Well, who was it?

1033
00:59:20,739 --> 00:59:25,098
Look, I've heard what you say about PII
protecting sleazy government.

1034
00:59:25,099 --> 00:59:27,858
If you weren't a junior
helplessly out of her depth,

1035
00:59:27,859 --> 00:59:30,378
I'd stop this trial
and report you to the Bar Council.

1036
00:59:30,379 --> 00:59:31,498
That's not fair.

1037
00:59:31,499 --> 00:59:34,258
That's another tack
you got away with at five.

1038
00:59:34,259 --> 00:59:36,178
This is not father
and daughter, Charlie.

1039
00:59:36,179 --> 00:59:39,298
Learn to listen for clues
instead of getting emotional.

1040
00:59:39,299 --> 00:59:40,699
What clues?

1041
00:59:49,739 --> 00:59:53,538
One of the witnesses as
unwittingly told you the name.

1042
00:59:53,539 --> 00:59:54,609
Who?

1043
00:59:55,299 --> 00:59:56,738
A witness who's given evidence?

1044
00:59:56,739 --> 00:59:58,418
I've already told you
more than you deserve.

1045
00:59:58,419 --> 01:00:00,778
Now get your defence back
on a proper footing.

1046
01:00:00,779 --> 01:00:02,538
What is it, by the way?

1047
01:00:02,539 --> 01:00:05,206
Mind your own business, my lord.

1048
01:00:07,499 --> 01:00:10,138
Mr Coward, or whatever your name is?

1049
01:00:10,139 --> 01:00:11,578
My name isn't Coward.

1050
01:00:11,579 --> 01:00:14,378
Miss Deed, I've warned you.
If you call him Mr Coward again,

1051
01:00:14,379 --> 01:00:17,046
I'll instruct him not to answer.

1052
01:00:17,539 --> 01:00:22,789
Mr X, did you know the bomb
was being planted at the university?

1053
01:00:22,939 --> 01:00:24,856
I knew of a bomb.

1054
01:00:26,419 --> 01:00:28,978
Who told you it was being planted?

1055
01:00:28,979 --> 01:00:31,058
The defendant, I believe.

1056
01:00:31,059 --> 01:00:34,378
Charlie, he doesn't sound sure.

1057
01:00:34,379 --> 01:00:36,179
You don't sound sure.

1058
01:00:36,699 --> 01:00:38,858
I am sure it was him.

1059
01:00:38,859 --> 01:00:40,179
Charlie...

1060
01:00:42,459 --> 01:00:44,858
My lord, are we to know
who is conducting this defence?

1061
01:00:44,859 --> 01:00:47,098
That would be something to know.

1062
01:00:47,099 --> 01:00:49,298
I'm conferring with a colleague.

1063
01:00:49,299 --> 01:00:52,218
I wonder if the public purse
can stretch so far?

1064
01:00:52,219 --> 01:00:54,378
I have another case in 10 days' time.

1065
01:00:54,379 --> 01:00:56,098
There's no price on justice.

1066
01:00:56,099 --> 01:01:02,433
Yes, I agree. But we don't all want
to serve a life sentence waiting for it.

1067
01:01:08,379 --> 01:01:10,546
When did he tell you this?

1068
01:01:11,019 --> 01:01:13,178
I don't exactly remember.

1069
01:01:13,179 --> 01:01:16,263
Do you exactly remember what he said?

1070
01:01:17,419 --> 01:01:22,169
It was something like,
"We plan to bomb the new building."

1071
01:01:25,859 --> 01:01:27,498
You recorded this, did you?

1072
01:01:27,499 --> 01:01:29,219
No, I didn't.

1073
01:01:29,699 --> 01:01:32,458
Did you not have the means
to record conversations?

1074
01:01:32,459 --> 01:01:34,709
It was too dangerous.

1075
01:01:36,499 --> 01:01:38,298
You claim your paymaster

1076
01:01:38,299 --> 01:01:41,018
sent you to infiltrate
the Animal Rights Movement

1077
01:01:41,019 --> 01:01:46,769
but you didn't record the defendant
making the most damning statement?

1078
01:01:49,419 --> 01:01:51,169
I didn't, it's true.

1079
01:01:52,019 --> 01:01:55,686
Then you're not only a coward,
you're a fool.

1080
01:01:56,579 --> 01:01:59,413
Charlie, Charlie.

1081
01:02:04,539 --> 01:02:05,859
Miss Deed.

1082
01:02:09,939 --> 01:02:12,738
Mr X Files,
do you know what was to happen

1083
01:02:12,739 --> 01:02:15,458
to the animals in the building
that was bombed?

1084
01:02:15,459 --> 01:02:17,289
Of course I do.

1085
01:02:18,739 --> 01:02:20,898
The would have been looked after.

1086
01:02:20,899 --> 01:02:23,233
Almost as if it was a hotel.

1087
01:02:24,699 --> 01:02:27,338
Did you learn that
from the Animal Rights Movement?

1088
01:02:27,339 --> 01:02:28,538
No, it's a known fact.

1089
01:02:28,539 --> 01:02:32,498
The animals would get coffee
and croissants and Sunday papers?

1090
01:02:32,499 --> 01:02:33,618
My lord...

1091
01:02:33,619 --> 01:02:35,869
Look, how is this relevant?

1092
01:02:35,939 --> 01:02:39,178
This man claims that he infiltrated
the defendant's organisation.

1093
01:02:39,179 --> 01:02:41,929
Yet, he knows little key details.

1094
01:02:42,299 --> 01:02:46,883
This goes to the credibility
of all his other testimony.

1095
01:02:47,299 --> 01:02:49,818
Charlie, it's going well.

1096
01:02:59,259 --> 01:03:00,778
Miss Deed, I'm growing weary

1097
01:03:00,779 --> 01:03:03,698
of your constantly consulting
our junior.

1098
01:03:03,699 --> 01:03:06,033
Who is running this defence?

1099
01:03:06,099 --> 01:03:09,099
Mr X, will you look at these photos?

1100
01:03:16,299 --> 01:03:18,818
Are these some of the experiments
that your friends have conducted?

1101
01:03:18,819 --> 01:03:20,578
- My lord, this is scandalous.
- Yes, I agree.

1102
01:03:20,579 --> 01:03:22,578
- Monkeys, with their eyes sewn up?
- No, no, no.

1103
01:03:22,579 --> 01:03:24,578
- Electrodes in their brains?
- Stop now.

1104
01:03:24,579 --> 01:03:26,498
Are you happy that
this is done in your name?

1105
01:03:26,499 --> 01:03:28,059
I said, enough!

1106
01:03:40,659 --> 01:03:41,978
Members of the jury,

1107
01:03:41,979 --> 01:03:46,313
since moral justification
is not a defence to murder,

1108
01:03:46,379 --> 01:03:49,538
I fail to see the relevance
of these photographs,

1109
01:03:49,539 --> 01:03:51,658
so you must disregard them.

1110
01:03:51,659 --> 01:03:53,738
Now, I'm going to rise
for a short time now,

1111
01:03:53,739 --> 01:03:58,489
so it's more of that
frightful tea and coffee, I'm afraid.

1112
01:04:04,819 --> 01:04:08,153
Mr Free, you can go down for the moment.

1113
01:04:09,099 --> 01:04:12,978
Miss Deed, you stay right where you are.
I'll be back.

1114
01:04:12,979 --> 01:04:14,579
All rise.

1115
01:04:14,699 --> 01:04:16,698
Am I being too hard on Charlie?

1116
01:04:16,699 --> 01:04:19,339
Well, it's not for me to say.

1117
01:04:19,419 --> 01:04:22,098
Well, you have plenty to say
about everting else.

1118
01:04:22,099 --> 01:04:23,933
Find Mrs Mills for me.

1119
01:04:27,459 --> 01:04:29,538
Just what do you think
you're doing in my court?

1120
01:04:29,539 --> 01:04:32,298
Guiding Charlie through this quagmire
that you're creating for her.

1121
01:04:32,299 --> 01:04:35,058
- She should know better.
- She is defending her client.

1122
01:04:35,059 --> 01:04:37,418
She is being stupid and emotional.

1123
01:04:37,419 --> 01:04:39,858
Well, try being as dispassionate
in your treatment of her.

1124
01:04:39,859 --> 01:04:41,938
I'm trying to bring some dignity...

1125
01:04:41,939 --> 01:04:43,218
Not from where I'm sitting.

1126
01:04:43,219 --> 01:04:45,136
You shouldn't be there.

1127
01:04:45,339 --> 01:04:47,378
Giving her stupid advice.

1128
01:04:47,379 --> 01:04:50,129
You self-righteous, arrogant man.

1129
01:04:50,619 --> 01:04:53,618
You shouldn't be conducting
this travesty as you are.

1130
01:04:53,619 --> 01:04:56,378
Letting that wretched Mr X
give evidence from behind a screen?

1131
01:04:56,379 --> 01:04:57,458
It's a valid process.

1132
01:04:57,459 --> 01:05:00,098
It stinks of unjust
management of evidence.

1133
01:05:00,099 --> 01:05:01,378
Yes, well, you better get used to it.

1134
01:05:01,379 --> 01:05:02,898
Because it's going
to be used more and more

1135
01:05:02,899 --> 01:05:04,418
if government ministers have their way.

1136
01:05:04,419 --> 01:05:05,698
Shame on you, John.

1137
01:05:05,699 --> 01:05:07,658
At one time you were the bulwark
against such iniquity.

1138
01:05:07,659 --> 01:05:10,538
Now, you are being
as cowardly as that witness

1139
01:05:10,539 --> 01:05:12,873
hiding behind his anonymity.

1140
01:05:13,059 --> 01:05:17,058
I can't help you, Jo.
no matter how much I love you.

1141
01:05:17,059 --> 01:05:18,858
Oh, you don't love anyone but yourself.

1142
01:05:18,859 --> 01:05:22,693
What are you doing
with this surgeon?

1143
01:05:22,859 --> 01:05:24,498
Is it Michael? Is he the attraction?

1144
01:05:24,499 --> 01:05:27,338
Yeah, so you think I can't fall in love
with anyone other than you?

1145
01:05:27,339 --> 01:05:29,339
- Do you love him?
- Yes.

1146
01:05:30,619 --> 01:05:33,698
I don't know, I think so.
I'm thinking about it.

1147
01:05:33,699 --> 01:05:35,858
You don't know,
you're thinking about it?

1148
01:05:35,859 --> 01:05:38,699
Life is a lot more fun with Marc.

1149
01:05:38,779 --> 01:05:40,418
I'm not constantly in his shadow.

1150
01:05:40,419 --> 01:05:44,098
Well, if that's your problem,
push yourself harder.

1151
01:05:44,099 --> 01:05:45,698
Don't slip into the B-stream.

1152
01:05:45,699 --> 01:05:48,783
- Go to hell.
- Oh, I'm already there.

1153
01:05:57,099 --> 01:05:59,979
Rita, what's happening?

1154
01:06:00,059 --> 01:06:02,098
Are we coming back
before the lunch adjournment or what?

1155
01:06:02,099 --> 01:06:04,766
I'm not about to ask him, Brian.

1156
01:06:04,979 --> 01:06:06,459
I assume not.

1157
01:06:07,219 --> 01:06:09,578
Are you worried?
You're very quiet at the moment.

1158
01:06:09,579 --> 01:06:12,498
You know what the job's like.

1159
01:06:12,499 --> 01:06:14,916
Well, he said to the witness.

1160
01:06:15,339 --> 01:06:19,258
"Tell me, Doctor,
as a result of your examination,

1161
01:06:19,259 --> 01:06:23,578
"can you, um, tell us whether or not
the young lady is pregnant?"

1162
01:06:23,579 --> 01:06:26,258
And the doctor replied,
"Well, yes, she is.

1163
01:06:26,259 --> 01:06:29,593
"But not as a result of my examination."

1164
01:06:30,019 --> 01:06:31,249
Ah, John.

1165
01:06:31,699 --> 01:06:34,258
I hear you've got
women trouble in court.

1166
01:06:34,259 --> 01:06:35,379
Some.

1167
01:06:35,779 --> 01:06:39,418
- Do you have children, Morag?
- No, unfortunately.

1168
01:06:39,419 --> 01:06:41,458
Why do women feel that way?

1169
01:06:41,459 --> 01:06:42,978
Do you have children, Monty?

1170
01:06:42,979 --> 01:06:46,099
A son, last heard of in East Africa.

1171
01:06:46,179 --> 01:06:50,096
I'm expecting lurid tabloid headlines
an da now.

1172
01:06:50,739 --> 01:06:53,138
My daughter married a plumber.
Much good that did us.

1173
01:06:53,139 --> 01:06:56,218
He's too bus even to come round
to mend a tap washer for us.

1174
01:06:56,219 --> 01:06:57,378
Are you sitting here, Morag?

1175
01:06:57,379 --> 01:07:00,298
Yes, Monty's asked me
to look at a shaken baby case.

1176
01:07:00,299 --> 01:07:02,058
Some curious forensics.

1177
01:07:02,059 --> 01:07:04,726
Not interesting enough for Vera?

1178
01:07:05,259 --> 01:07:08,978
I thought it was more suited
to Morag's specialised knowledge.

1179
01:07:08,979 --> 01:07:12,098
I don't know why the don't hear
these cases in the family division.

1180
01:07:12,099 --> 01:07:14,098
Well, did you put that up
to the Lord Chancellor?

1181
01:07:14,099 --> 01:07:15,819
No, far too logical.

1182
01:07:17,459 --> 01:07:20,258
Would you like tea after you adjourn?

1183
01:07:20,259 --> 01:07:24,378
I'd like to hear some more about
your ideas for the family division.

1184
01:07:24,379 --> 01:07:25,499
Fine.

1185
01:07:40,259 --> 01:07:43,459
- Oh, you got some lunch, then.
- Yeah.

1186
01:07:43,619 --> 01:07:45,138
Sir Peter brought me a sandwich.

1187
01:07:45,139 --> 01:07:46,889
That was nice of him.

1188
01:07:46,899 --> 01:07:48,499
Yeah.

1189
01:07:48,819 --> 01:07:52,153
You know it's contempt, eating in court.

1190
01:07:54,219 --> 01:07:56,053
Cooled down, have you?

1191
01:07:56,939 --> 01:07:59,439
I was only doing my job, Dad.

1192
01:07:59,539 --> 01:08:00,618
Not very well, Chaz.

1193
01:08:00,619 --> 01:08:02,058
That's not what Jo said.

1194
01:08:02,059 --> 01:08:03,698
She's advising you badly.

1195
01:08:03,699 --> 01:08:05,338
Yeah, well, at least she is advising me.

1196
01:08:05,339 --> 01:08:07,839
Your client will be the loser.

1197
01:08:09,059 --> 01:08:12,226
Then tell me the name of that witness.

1198
01:08:14,579 --> 01:08:16,339
You know it already.

1199
01:08:17,339 --> 01:08:22,339
Stop posturing and try forensically
dissecting the witnesses.

1200
01:08:26,019 --> 01:08:27,089
How?

1201
01:08:27,779 --> 01:08:30,938
That's what being a junior
is all about, Chaz.

1202
01:08:30,939 --> 01:08:32,219
Learning.

1203
01:08:33,579 --> 01:08:37,246
Tell the usher
when you're read to apologise.

1204
01:08:43,219 --> 01:08:45,369
- Jo!
- Are you all right?

1205
01:08:45,459 --> 01:08:48,378
Yeah, yeah. Dad just told me
a witness said the name.

1206
01:08:48,379 --> 01:08:49,938
In court? Well, who?

1207
01:08:49,939 --> 01:08:51,773
He gave no other clue.

1208
01:08:52,699 --> 01:08:55,458
Why don't you see if you can persuade
them to give you the court transcript?

1209
01:08:55,459 --> 01:08:56,698
Dozens of names ave
already been mentioned.

1210
01:08:56,699 --> 01:08:59,949
Man of those to people in the movement.

1211
01:08:59,979 --> 01:09:01,896
Let's go and see Henry.

1212
01:09:03,379 --> 01:09:05,218
He's definitely not one of us.

1213
01:09:05,219 --> 01:09:06,698
It's just some scum

1214
01:09:06,699 --> 01:09:10,178
who's pieced together some bits
of information, that's all.

1215
01:09:10,179 --> 01:09:13,098
Was there anyone
who consistently evaded arrest?

1216
01:09:13,099 --> 01:09:14,858
Well, me. Up until now.

1217
01:09:14,859 --> 01:09:16,538
Take this seriously, Henry.

1218
01:09:16,539 --> 01:09:19,218
It can only be one of
a few people close to you.

1219
01:09:19,219 --> 01:09:21,179
I'd never believe that.

1220
01:09:21,739 --> 01:09:24,578
Did you know that Professor
Blackthorn ad cancer?

1221
01:09:24,579 --> 01:09:27,619
- No, I didn't. Did he?
- Mmm.

1222
01:09:30,899 --> 01:09:32,579
David Baxter knew.

1223
01:09:35,499 --> 01:09:38,458
With your leave, my lord,
I should like to call Moira Blackthorn,

1224
01:09:38,459 --> 01:09:41,043
the wife of the dead scientist.

1225
01:09:41,219 --> 01:09:43,458
My lord, is this witness
material to events

1226
01:09:43,459 --> 01:09:46,898
or merely being used
as an emotional lever on the jury?

1227
01:09:46,899 --> 01:09:50,316
I've no idea.
Let's hear her and find out.

1228
01:09:55,459 --> 01:09:58,818
Mrs Blackthorn, are you the widow
of Professor Clive Blackthorn,

1229
01:09:58,819 --> 01:10:01,338
who was murdered
in the university bombing?

1230
01:10:01,339 --> 01:10:03,923
Yes. Yes, I am.

1231
01:10:05,179 --> 01:10:08,698
Can you tell the jury ow
long you were married for?

1232
01:10:08,699 --> 01:10:11,619
Twenty-six years. At university.

1233
01:10:12,899 --> 01:10:15,066
Do you know the defendant?

1234
01:10:16,819 --> 01:10:19,138
Yes, he murdered my husband.

1235
01:10:19,139 --> 01:10:22,018
Mrs Blackthorn,
do you know that as a fact?

1236
01:10:22,019 --> 01:10:25,936
I know what he did to my husband
over the years.

1237
01:10:26,219 --> 01:10:28,969
I'm so angry about Clive's death.

1238
01:10:30,299 --> 01:10:32,799
Yes, I understand. Sir Peter?

1239
01:10:33,659 --> 01:10:36,659
How do you know the defendant?

1240
01:10:37,059 --> 01:10:39,418
He hounded my husband to his death.

1241
01:10:39,419 --> 01:10:42,859
He made our lives a complete misery.

1242
01:10:43,899 --> 01:10:46,816
We lived in terror the whole while.

1243
01:10:48,819 --> 01:10:53,236
How man times did Mr Free
harass you, Mrs Blackthorn?

1244
01:10:53,659 --> 01:10:56,698
It never stopped.
That's what made Clive so ill.

1245
01:10:56,699 --> 01:10:58,218
Can you be specific?

1246
01:10:58,219 --> 01:11:02,886
He brought my husband to his deathbed.
Isn't that enough?

1247
01:11:03,539 --> 01:11:07,738
I understand that this is painful,
but you have to be specific.

1248
01:11:07,739 --> 01:11:10,698
A young man's liberty depends on this.

1249
01:11:10,699 --> 01:11:13,199
I hope he's put away forever.

1250
01:11:13,419 --> 01:11:18,503
First, we have to show that he made
other than legal protests.

1251
01:11:19,219 --> 01:11:22,058
My husband hated them for what the did.

1252
01:11:22,059 --> 01:11:24,418
What about the lives
of the animals he experimented on?

1253
01:11:24,419 --> 01:11:25,779
My lord...

1254
01:11:28,139 --> 01:11:31,473
Are you comparing animals to my husband?

1255
01:11:32,899 --> 01:11:34,816
The have feelings, too.

1256
01:11:37,579 --> 01:11:39,409
The were what he did.

1257
01:11:39,739 --> 01:11:42,818
Clive was finding cures for
all kinds of illness, including his own.

1258
01:11:42,819 --> 01:11:46,418
Those disgusting people
stopped all that vital research.

1259
01:11:46,419 --> 01:11:47,819
What people?

1260
01:11:48,059 --> 01:11:49,699
Him and his kind.

1261
01:12:06,779 --> 01:12:10,196
What sort of cancer
did your husband have?

1262
01:12:13,939 --> 01:12:15,339
Liver sarcoma.

1263
01:12:15,419 --> 01:12:17,898
How seriously ill was he
at the time of his death?

1264
01:12:17,899 --> 01:12:20,258
My lord, I fail to see
that this has an bearing.

1265
01:12:20,259 --> 01:12:22,378
Not immediately clear, is it?

1266
01:12:22,379 --> 01:12:25,463
Quickly come to the point, Miss Deed.

1267
01:12:25,899 --> 01:12:27,419
Mrs Blackthorn?

1268
01:12:27,859 --> 01:12:31,609
He was very, very ill.
Made worse by his kind.

1269
01:12:32,619 --> 01:12:35,036
Only his work kept him going.

1270
01:12:35,499 --> 01:12:38,219
So, he knew that he was ding

1271
01:12:38,619 --> 01:12:40,658
- when he ran into the building.
- My lord...

1272
01:12:40,659 --> 01:12:43,498
Of course he knew.
He'd known for a long time.

1273
01:12:43,499 --> 01:12:46,338
My lord, ma I make
an unusual request at this point

1274
01:12:46,339 --> 01:12:50,178
and reserve my cross-examination
until Chief Inspector Hurst is recalled?

1275
01:12:50,179 --> 01:12:53,013
I have some additional questions.

1276
01:12:54,339 --> 01:12:57,018
Well, that's two applications,
but all right.

1277
01:12:57,019 --> 01:13:00,418
Let's get her back,
assuming Sir Peter has no objections.

1278
01:13:00,419 --> 01:13:06,586
Mrs Blackthorn, would you mind going
back to the witness room for a moment?

1279
01:13:07,379 --> 01:13:08,578
Where is this going, Jo?

1280
01:13:08,579 --> 01:13:10,658
Motive. There's something here.

1281
01:13:10,659 --> 01:13:13,658
His running back into the building
doesn't make sense.

1282
01:13:13,659 --> 01:13:15,826
Try to uncover why he did.

1283
01:13:16,419 --> 01:13:21,178
Chief Inspector, can you come back
for the moment, please?

1284
01:13:21,179 --> 01:13:23,263
You are still under oath.

1285
01:13:23,659 --> 01:13:24,979
Miss Deed?

1286
01:13:25,619 --> 01:13:26,858
Chief Inspector Hurst,

1287
01:13:26,859 --> 01:13:29,858
when Professor Blackthorn
broke through the police cordon

1288
01:13:29,859 --> 01:13:32,218
and ran back into the building,

1289
01:13:32,219 --> 01:13:34,298
what was his state of mind?

1290
01:13:34,299 --> 01:13:37,018
It was hard to say. It was all so fast.

1291
01:13:37,019 --> 01:13:42,019
You're a police officer.
You assess situations in an instant.

1292
01:13:42,939 --> 01:13:44,739
He was very agitated.

1293
01:13:45,619 --> 01:13:48,786
Like a man who'd forgotten his papers?

1294
01:13:49,459 --> 01:13:52,043
He was more agitated than that.

1295
01:13:52,859 --> 01:13:54,619
Did he say anything?

1296
01:13:55,139 --> 01:13:57,723
I think he said, "Forgive me."

1297
01:13:59,379 --> 01:14:02,129
He was asking you to forgive him?

1298
01:14:02,219 --> 01:14:03,738
I don't think it was me.

1299
01:14:03,739 --> 01:14:06,489
Did he call out to God or anyone?

1300
01:14:06,539 --> 01:14:09,623
Well, not unless God is called Davis.

1301
01:14:10,019 --> 01:14:11,779
That's what he said.

1302
01:14:16,539 --> 01:14:17,873
Miss Deed?

1303
01:14:21,659 --> 01:14:23,743
Could it have been David?

1304
01:14:27,299 --> 01:14:29,738
It ma have been. I don't know.

1305
01:14:29,739 --> 01:14:33,498
My lord, ma I resume
cross-examining Mrs Blackthorn?

1306
01:14:33,499 --> 01:14:37,749
Perhaps Sir Peter wishes
to re-examine this witness.

1307
01:14:40,259 --> 01:14:43,978
What was Professor Blackthorn
working on at the time?

1308
01:14:43,979 --> 01:14:46,498
A cure for Parkinson's disease.

1309
01:14:46,499 --> 01:14:50,258
Wouldn't you say, Chief Inspector,
that that was of very great importance?

1310
01:14:50,259 --> 01:14:55,426
My lord, Chief Inspector Hurst
is not qualified to answer that.

1311
01:14:56,779 --> 01:14:59,029
Thank you, Chief Inspector.

1312
01:15:03,459 --> 01:15:08,219
Mrs Blackthorn, did your husband have
any sympathy at all

1313
01:15:08,299 --> 01:15:11,133
with the animal rights supporters?

1314
01:15:11,899 --> 01:15:14,138
- He could have killed them.
- As he did the animals?

1315
01:15:14,139 --> 01:15:15,459
Miss Deed.

1316
01:15:17,139 --> 01:15:18,659
Did he hate them?

1317
01:15:18,739 --> 01:15:19,858
He loathed them. He felt

1318
01:15:19,859 --> 01:15:23,618
the stopped him from getting
a Nobel Prize for medicine.

1319
01:15:23,619 --> 01:15:25,938
He wanted to smash them, didn't he?

1320
01:15:25,939 --> 01:15:27,939
The had ruined his life,

1321
01:15:28,539 --> 01:15:29,898
his work, his health.

1322
01:15:29,899 --> 01:15:31,898
But he was more angry
that everyone seemed so powerless

1323
01:15:31,899 --> 01:15:36,698
to do anything about these people.
not the college, not the police. No one.

1324
01:15:36,699 --> 01:15:38,298
Did he have police protection?

1325
01:15:38,299 --> 01:15:41,818
No, of course he didn't.
The didn't offer that.

1326
01:15:41,819 --> 01:15:43,458
The police must have tried to help him.

1327
01:15:43,459 --> 01:15:45,209
No, the were useless.

1328
01:15:45,219 --> 01:15:48,138
I can't believe that. You're saying
these people were terrorists.

1329
01:15:48,139 --> 01:15:51,889
No, it's true. It's true.
The gave us no help.

1330
01:15:53,339 --> 01:15:56,338
Well, only one young man was of an use.

1331
01:15:56,339 --> 01:15:58,658
Clive said he was trying
to infiltrate their wretched cell.

1332
01:15:58,659 --> 01:16:00,178
Who was that?

1333
01:16:00,179 --> 01:16:03,299
Um... I think he worked for MI5.

1334
01:16:03,379 --> 01:16:06,629
What can one man do against terrorists?

1335
01:16:06,859 --> 01:16:09,779
- He worked for MI5.
- MI5?

1336
01:16:11,139 --> 01:16:15,056
That's not true.
Why didn't he stop the bombing?

1337
01:16:15,419 --> 01:16:17,336
You'll have to ask him.

1338
01:16:20,819 --> 01:16:25,178
Mrs Blackthorn, how advanced
was your husband's cancer?

1339
01:16:25,179 --> 01:16:28,019
Very. The doctors were amazed.

1340
01:16:28,259 --> 01:16:29,618
He should have been dead

1341
01:16:29,619 --> 01:16:32,338
but Clive refused
to let those people beat him.

1342
01:16:32,339 --> 01:16:34,938
Was he on drugs for his pain?

1343
01:16:34,939 --> 01:16:37,418
Yes, of course, a whole cocktail.

1344
01:16:37,419 --> 01:16:42,179
So in his drug-pain state,
angrily blaming the protestors,

1345
01:16:42,699 --> 01:16:46,258
is it possible that your husband
committed suicide to get back at them?

1346
01:16:46,259 --> 01:16:47,818
My lord, this is so offensive.

1347
01:16:47,819 --> 01:16:49,258
Well, yes, I agree, Sir Peter,

1348
01:16:49,259 --> 01:16:51,898
but I can't prevent counsel
asking questions

1349
01:16:51,899 --> 01:16:54,149
on the grounds of delicacy.

1350
01:16:54,619 --> 01:16:58,258
I hope the jury will accept it
for what it is.

1351
01:16:58,259 --> 01:17:00,259
Mrs Blackthorn?

1352
01:17:00,779 --> 01:17:02,618
Clive was a fighter to the very end.

1353
01:17:02,619 --> 01:17:07,036
Did he ever mention
getting even with his persecutors?

1354
01:17:07,579 --> 01:17:10,058
Well, he would have done anything
to pay them back.

1355
01:17:10,059 --> 01:17:12,618
Even walk into the jaws of death?

1356
01:17:12,619 --> 01:17:16,218
He was already ding.
The had more or less killed him.

1357
01:17:16,219 --> 01:17:18,058
Could he have been helped by MI5?

1358
01:17:18,059 --> 01:17:19,289
My lord?

1359
01:17:20,819 --> 01:17:22,578
He was the only one
who tried to help.

1360
01:17:22,579 --> 01:17:24,338
Did he help him to end it?

1361
01:17:24,339 --> 01:17:26,423
My husband was suffering.

1362
01:17:26,659 --> 01:17:28,993
Did he say his name?

1363
01:17:29,819 --> 01:17:32,986
I don't remember if he did.

1364
01:17:33,459 --> 01:17:37,249
Does David Baxter sound familiar?

1365
01:17:37,339 --> 01:17:38,699
My lord...

1366
01:17:38,779 --> 01:17:43,298
Well, you're the one
who's just confirmed it, Sir Peter.

1367
01:17:43,299 --> 01:17:45,498
It is scandalous his name has come out.

1368
01:17:45,499 --> 01:17:46,858
It was his PII witness.

1369
01:17:46,859 --> 01:17:48,539
Miss Deed, enough.

1370
01:17:50,459 --> 01:17:53,458
Mrs Blackthorn,
please think carefully about this.

1371
01:17:53,459 --> 01:17:56,938
As much as you despise
what this man stands for,

1372
01:17:56,939 --> 01:17:59,418
I'm sure you don't want
to see him wrongly imprisoned.

1373
01:17:59,419 --> 01:18:00,818
He killed my husband!

1374
01:18:00,819 --> 01:18:05,418
Is it possible, knowing he was ding
of inoperable cancer,

1375
01:18:05,419 --> 01:18:08,498
you husband made a plan
with the MI5 man,

1376
01:18:08,499 --> 01:18:11,618
plant the bomb in the building
to discredit the protestors?

1377
01:18:11,619 --> 01:18:14,058
- This is ludicrous.
- He cried out, "Forgive me,"

1378
01:18:14,059 --> 01:18:15,658
but then changed his mind.

1379
01:18:15,659 --> 01:18:16,978
- I cannot allow...
- I don't know.

1380
01:18:16,979 --> 01:18:18,058
It is possible though,
isn't it?

1381
01:18:18,059 --> 01:18:19,178
No, no...

1382
01:18:19,179 --> 01:18:21,138
I think you do know.

1383
01:18:21,139 --> 01:18:23,973
Yes, no... Clive... It's possible.

1384
01:18:29,179 --> 01:18:32,018
My lord, I would like to submit
that there's no case to answer,

1385
01:18:32,019 --> 01:18:34,378
on the grounds that
this is almost certainly

1386
01:18:34,379 --> 01:18:37,129
the work of an agent provocateur.

1387
01:18:40,619 --> 01:18:43,203
Very bad timing.

1388
01:18:43,619 --> 01:18:45,138
Will the jury
get themselves some more tea.

1389
01:18:45,139 --> 01:18:48,618
I'll see counsel in chambers.
You'd better come too, Mrs Mills.

1390
01:18:48,619 --> 01:18:50,219
All rise.

1391
01:18:57,299 --> 01:18:59,383
Have you learned nothing?

1392
01:18:59,579 --> 01:19:01,578
You can't let MI5 get away with this.

1393
01:19:01,579 --> 01:19:04,818
The planted the bomb at the university
or helped the professor.

1394
01:19:04,819 --> 01:19:07,458
Mystical divination is now
one of your man talents, is it?

1395
01:19:07,459 --> 01:19:10,658
MI5's role, official or unofficial,
needs looking at, John.

1396
01:19:10,659 --> 01:19:12,743
This is preposterous, Jo.

1397
01:19:13,299 --> 01:19:16,098
Whatever their role,
by behaving as you did in court,

1398
01:19:16,099 --> 01:19:19,349
you have jeopardised this entire trial.

1399
01:19:19,419 --> 01:19:21,738
I should discharge the jury
and order a retrial.

1400
01:19:21,739 --> 01:19:24,498
- That's not fair.
- It's called the consequences.

1401
01:19:24,499 --> 01:19:25,898
She was right to raise this.

1402
01:19:25,899 --> 01:19:28,209
Not in front of the jury.

1403
01:19:28,299 --> 01:19:31,498
It would be most unfortunate
if we had to try this again.

1404
01:19:31,499 --> 01:19:33,939
Yes. And if we do,

1405
01:19:34,339 --> 01:19:36,738
I'd be obliged to report you
to the Bar Council

1406
01:19:36,739 --> 01:19:38,698
and make a heft wasted costs order.

1407
01:19:38,699 --> 01:19:39,858
That seems a might harsh.

1408
01:19:39,859 --> 01:19:42,443
Well, perhaps you'd like to pay.

1409
01:19:42,939 --> 01:19:45,218
As the barrister
attempting to advise her.

1410
01:19:45,219 --> 01:19:46,579
Attempting?

1411
01:19:47,779 --> 01:19:50,779
Yes, my lord. Why don't you do that?

1412
01:19:52,299 --> 01:19:58,133
I'll reflect on this overnight.
You'll have my decision in the morning.

1413
01:20:08,379 --> 01:20:09,379
Well?

1414
01:20:09,499 --> 01:20:12,166
It's not for me to comment, sir.

1415
01:20:31,979 --> 01:20:33,339
Yes!

1416
01:20:35,779 --> 01:20:37,696
Sounds like a big bear.

1417
01:20:40,259 --> 01:20:45,618
My daughter has been trying
to turn my court into a bear pit.

1418
01:20:45,619 --> 01:20:47,818
I hear she drove the Attorney General
back to the Commons.

1419
01:20:47,819 --> 01:20:50,403
She has behaved very foolishly.

1420
01:20:51,579 --> 01:20:52,729
Maybe.

1421
01:21:03,419 --> 01:21:05,299
- Hello.
- It's me.

1422
01:21:05,379 --> 01:21:06,379
Okay.

1423
01:21:21,139 --> 01:21:22,298
Is anyone else here?

1424
01:21:22,299 --> 01:21:23,418
What, apart from Mimi?

1425
01:21:23,419 --> 01:21:24,939
A human female?

1426
01:21:26,579 --> 01:21:29,246
Why don't you check the bedroom?

1427
01:21:33,179 --> 01:21:37,429
I don't want anyone to hear
what I am about to say.

1428
01:21:38,699 --> 01:21:41,858
You behave like
a petulant, spoilt child.

1429
01:21:41,859 --> 01:21:43,498
Yes, Charlie spoke out of turn,

1430
01:21:43,499 --> 01:21:45,698
but only because she was
passionate and enthusiastic

1431
01:21:45,699 --> 01:21:47,858
about her client's innocence.

1432
01:21:47,859 --> 01:21:49,698
What you tried to do
was push that out of her.

1433
01:21:49,699 --> 01:21:52,498
If you do that,
you will have killed something

1434
01:21:52,499 --> 01:21:55,858
that your profession
is so sorely lacking.

1435
01:21:55,859 --> 01:21:57,378
And then we're all left with

1436
01:21:57,379 --> 01:22:00,138
is the cynical and the jaded
and the money-grabbing.

1437
01:22:00,139 --> 01:22:02,418
So why don't you think about that
before you go into court

1438
01:22:02,419 --> 01:22:04,836
and throw your weight around?

1439
01:22:06,339 --> 01:22:09,898
- Finished?
- No, actually, I haven't. Not quite.

1440
01:22:09,899 --> 01:22:12,138
You can report me, I don't care.

1441
01:22:12,139 --> 01:22:14,698
The way the profession's going,
I'm not sure if I want to be part of it.

1442
01:22:14,699 --> 01:22:17,418
But you should nurture
what Charlie's got.

1443
01:22:17,419 --> 01:22:23,018
Yes, yes, she can be gauche at times.
But she has wonderful qualities.

1444
01:22:23,019 --> 01:22:27,519
So just remember that
when you make out your complaint.

1445
01:22:34,499 --> 01:22:36,583
Sir Ian Rochester, Judge.

1446
01:22:37,139 --> 01:22:40,056
- Good morning, Ian.
- Good morning.

1447
01:22:40,459 --> 01:22:42,498
The Home Secretary
approached the Lord Chancellor

1448
01:22:42,499 --> 01:22:45,938
about the informant who penetrated
the Animal Rights Movement.

1449
01:22:45,939 --> 01:22:48,458
He was outed I'm afraid, Ian.
Sit down.

1450
01:22:48,459 --> 01:22:51,376
We understand his face wasn't seen.

1451
01:22:51,419 --> 01:22:56,058
It would be greatly appreciated
if his face was not revealed.

1452
01:22:56,059 --> 01:22:58,976
By Haughton or the Lord Chancellor?

1453
01:23:02,699 --> 01:23:06,378
I believe your daughter
ma have caused a possible retrial?

1454
01:23:06,379 --> 01:23:07,449
Mmm.

1455
01:23:08,579 --> 01:23:10,829
I have to respond to that.

1456
01:23:10,899 --> 01:23:13,218
You're not thinking of reporting her
to the Bar Council?

1457
01:23:13,219 --> 01:23:15,498
She did behave inappropriately.

1458
01:23:15,499 --> 01:23:20,499
If you poison that well, John,
it stays tainted a long while.

1459
01:23:21,419 --> 01:23:24,218
This is a side of you
I've not seen before, Ian.

1460
01:23:24,219 --> 01:23:25,419
I know.

1461
01:23:26,539 --> 01:23:28,339
Strife is so wearing.

1462
01:23:43,779 --> 01:23:44,849
Keeping busy?

1463
01:23:46,059 --> 01:23:48,218
Waiting for the axe to fall.

1464
01:23:48,219 --> 01:23:49,819
No Mrs Mills?

1465
01:23:49,899 --> 01:23:51,858
She probably doesn't want
to be associated with me.

1466
01:23:51,859 --> 01:23:53,898
I'm sure it's not as bad as all that.

1467
01:23:53,899 --> 01:23:56,618
I thought you made
a very good fist of the defence.

1468
01:23:56,619 --> 01:24:00,286
I've seen a lot of leaders
perform less well.

1469
01:24:00,819 --> 01:24:03,403
We'll know soon enough, anyway.

1470
01:24:05,859 --> 01:24:07,459
All rise.

1471
01:24:18,139 --> 01:24:20,898
Members of the jury,
thank you for your patience.

1472
01:24:20,899 --> 01:24:22,698
It must be very frustrating for you,

1473
01:24:22,699 --> 01:24:26,533
having to go out
ever time things get exciting.

1474
01:24:26,779 --> 01:24:29,859
The defence has submitted to me

1475
01:24:29,939 --> 01:24:32,058
that a charge of murder
cannot be established

1476
01:24:32,059 --> 01:24:34,059
on the Crown's evidence.

1477
01:24:34,659 --> 01:24:38,938
The prosecution has insisted
that only the charge of murder

1478
01:24:38,939 --> 01:24:41,189
be considered in this case.

1479
01:24:41,259 --> 01:24:44,298
Now, that is a very grave charge
and quite rightly

1480
01:24:44,299 --> 01:24:46,938
requires proof that
the defendant did the killing,

1481
01:24:46,939 --> 01:24:51,439
along with the intention
to cause grievous bodily harm.

1482
01:24:52,539 --> 01:24:55,058
Now, I am not satisfied
on this evidence

1483
01:24:55,059 --> 01:24:57,258
that you could be sure of that.

1484
01:24:57,259 --> 01:25:00,098
So I'm taking the unusual step
of directing you

1485
01:25:00,099 --> 01:25:03,016
to return a verdict of "not guilt".

1486
01:25:04,339 --> 01:25:08,018
- Mr Harrison?
- As you with, my lord.

1487
01:25:08,019 --> 01:25:10,853
Will the jury foreman please rise?

1488
01:25:11,659 --> 01:25:15,178
Members of the jury,
upon his lordship's direction,

1489
01:25:15,179 --> 01:25:18,939
do you find the defendant
not guilt of murder?

1490
01:25:19,619 --> 01:25:20,689
Yes.

1491
01:25:21,099 --> 01:25:24,349
Is that
the verdict of you all?

1492
01:25:24,819 --> 01:25:28,736
Yes, but couldn't we
find him guilt of anything?

1493
01:25:29,539 --> 01:25:32,298
What would you like
to find him guilt of?

1494
01:25:32,299 --> 01:25:34,818
Well, he did cause
Professor Blackthorn lots of problems.

1495
01:25:34,819 --> 01:25:36,418
Yes, well, I'm sure he did.

1496
01:25:36,419 --> 01:25:39,753
I think it's called legitimate protest.

1497
01:25:39,779 --> 01:25:43,218
You see, we must be very careful
not to allow the law

1498
01:25:43,219 --> 01:25:45,553
to curtail political protest

1499
01:25:45,779 --> 01:25:49,898
just because it makes one section
of society feel uncomfortable.

1500
01:25:49,899 --> 01:25:52,858
Always provided, of course,
that that protest remains lawful.

1501
01:25:52,859 --> 01:25:55,693
Well, what about the man from MI5?

1502
01:25:56,099 --> 01:25:57,938
Would you like him charged instead?

1503
01:25:57,939 --> 01:26:01,523
Well, he seemed
to be involved in something.

1504
01:26:03,619 --> 01:26:06,953
Well, you are right to raise this issue.

1505
01:26:07,379 --> 01:26:10,058
I will ask
the Director of Public Prosecutions,

1506
01:26:10,059 --> 01:26:13,578
along with the Home Office,
to undertake an inquiry

1507
01:26:13,579 --> 01:26:17,329
into the alleged workings of MI5
in this case.

1508
01:26:18,099 --> 01:26:21,378
It would be a grave threat
to civil liberty and political freedom

1509
01:26:21,379 --> 01:26:24,698
if an member of the security services
were to act in a way

1510
01:26:24,699 --> 01:26:27,138
that has been suggested in this case.

1511
01:26:27,139 --> 01:26:32,556
I want the findings of that inquiry
reported back to me in court.

1512
01:26:34,099 --> 01:26:37,699
As for you, Mr Free, you are...
You can go.

1513
01:26:39,859 --> 01:26:42,898
Members of the jury, thank you
for your time and your patience.

1514
01:26:42,899 --> 01:26:44,899
You are also free to go.

1515
01:26:45,859 --> 01:26:47,499
All rise.

1516
01:26:57,819 --> 01:26:59,969
I'm outraged, Ian.

1517
01:27:00,299 --> 01:27:03,098
A High Court judge demanding that
a Home Secretary reports to him

1518
01:27:03,099 --> 01:27:07,178
the result of an inquiry
into the security service?

1519
01:27:07,179 --> 01:27:10,538
But what you now have
is a Deed who behaved improperly

1520
01:27:10,539 --> 01:27:12,778
in relation to his daughter.

1521
01:27:12,779 --> 01:27:14,618
That would be
very difficult for me, Ian.

1522
01:27:14,619 --> 01:27:16,378
She's George's daughter as well.

1523
01:27:16,379 --> 01:27:20,338
Perhaps I should ask the Attorney,
as head of the bar, to look into it.

1524
01:27:20,339 --> 01:27:22,756
That leaves your hands clean.

1525
01:27:33,499 --> 01:27:35,019
Charlie, Judge.

1526
01:27:38,459 --> 01:27:39,779
Excuse me.

1527
01:27:55,899 --> 01:28:00,659
I just wanted to say that I'm sorry
for embarrassing you.

1528
01:28:03,779 --> 01:28:07,778
Well, I must have got something right
with your upbringing.

1529
01:28:07,779 --> 01:28:11,058
Very few people
want to apologise nowadays.

1530
01:28:11,059 --> 01:28:13,976
Even when the are completely wrong.

1531
01:28:15,179 --> 01:28:17,763
Did I get it completely wrong?

1532
01:28:18,339 --> 01:28:19,498
You spoke out of turn.

1533
01:28:19,499 --> 01:28:20,938
I was trying to help my client.

1534
01:28:20,939 --> 01:28:23,938
Well, you sought the truth
for your client.

1535
01:28:23,939 --> 01:28:26,189
But you were too emotional.

1536
01:28:32,979 --> 01:28:34,579
So are you, Dad.

